const GW_DECKS = {"tox": {"name": "Principles of Toxicology", "n": 31}, "resp": {"name": "Toxic Responses", "n": 43}, "treat": {"name": "Treating Poisonings", "n": 36}, "pgx": {"name": "Introduction to Pharmacogenomics", "n": 42}, "pgxex": {"name": "Pharmacogenomic Examples", "n": 62}, "pgxres": {"name": "Pharmacogenomics Resources", "n": 22}};
const GUIDE_WALK = {"guide3-1": {"title": "Poison, toxin, toxicant and xenobiotic", "segs": [{"t": "Section one. Four words for harmful things. Toxicology is the study of poisons, and four words name something noxious. Poison is the general word, the layman's term, for anything that can cause a bad response. Toxin and toxicant divide harmful substances by who made them. A toxin is made by a biological system, a living organism. A toxicant is made by people, through human activity. Xenobiotic names any compound in an organism that the organism does not normally make or is not expected to contain, harmful or not. When a question describes a substance and asks for the one correct word, the deciding question is where the substance came from.", "s": "tox:6", "r": null}, {"t": "Snake venom is a toxin, because an animal makes it. She said, You could say it's poisonous, but technically it's a toxin. So an answer that calls snake venom a toxicant is wrong. Whatever is harmful in a smokestack emission is a toxicant, because human activity produces it. Whether the plume is harmful at all is unknown until someone samples it, because it could just be steam. She admitted that she often uses these words loosely in lecture, but she pointed out the difference on purpose, so the words are not interchangeable.", "s": "tox:6", "r": null}, {"t": "Medications belong on the list of toxic things. When she asked the class what is toxic, the answers included food, hormones, pollution, drugs, rat poison, venom and alcohol. A medication is toxic at a high enough dose, because the dose makes the poison. Xenobiotic is defined by being foreign to the organism, not by being harmful, so it overlaps every other term. A poison, a toxin, a toxicant, a food additive or a medication can each be a xenobiotic. That makes two answers wrong: that a xenobiotic is by definition toxic, and that medications are not poisons.", "s": "tox:4", "r": null}, {"t": "For a venomous animal, identifying the animal decides whether there is a toxin at all. The coral snake is very venomous, and its venom carries a neurotoxin. Its red bands touch yellow: red and yellow kills a fellow. The king snake has the same colors, but its red touches black: red and black, friend of Jack. The king snake is not poisonous, and people keep them as pets. Other organisms she named also make their own poisons, so they are sources of toxins. Oleander is poisonous in its leaves, flowers and roots, the pitohui is a bird that secretes a poison from its skin, and the male platypus has a spike on its back legs in the breeding season.", "s": "tox:5", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-2": {"title": "Five principles of toxicology", "segs": [{"t": "Section two. The five principles of toxicology. Toxicologists look at any harmful exposure through five principles, and each one is a question. First, source: where does the noxious substance come from? Second, exposure: how is the person or animal exposed? Third, A D M E T: what does the body do with it? Fourth, dose: is the amount high enough to cause a problem? Fifth, target and mechanism of action: where in the body does it act, and how does it cause harm? Her key for the reading and its discussion guide are both organized by these five.", "s": "tox:9", "r": null}, {"t": "The order follows the substance. It has to come from a source, then reach the body by some exposure. A D M E T then decides how much reaches each tissue and whether metabolism makes it more or less harmful. The dose decides whether that amount is enough to cause harm, and target and mechanism say where and how the harm happens. The principles also depend on each other. The route of exposure decides whether a drug passes through the liver first, which is metabolism, and the dose that reaches a target decides whether the target is harmed.", "s": "tox:9", "r": null}, {"t": "A D M E stands for absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion. She once said elimination aloud while expanding it, but the word in her key is excretion. The T is added in a toxicology course and stands for toxicity, because the same four processes now matter for how they produce or prevent harm. Route, duration, single versus repeated exposure, and frequency are parts of the exposure principle, not separate principles.", "s": "tox:9", "r": null}, {"t": "The fifth principle is the same idea as a drug's mechanism of action, but for harm. She said, sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target. That is why her practice quiz marks false the statement that the mechanism of a drug's toxicity is always the same as the mechanism of its efficacy. The fifth principle has two parts, target and mechanism of action. Target alone, or mechanism alone, is only half of it.", "s": "tox:28", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-3": {"title": "Sources of exposure", "segs": [{"t": "Section three. Source. The first principle asks where a noxious substance comes from. Toxicologists sort sources into four categories: environmental exposures, occupational exposures, critter bites and stings, and medications. Environmental means contaminants and pollution where people live and move, such as diesel fumes from a bus downtown, or living on ground built on a landfill where dust blows. Occupational means workplace exposures, such as a factory's stacks or a benzene leak. Critter bites and stings are venoms, from snakes, spiders or the platypus spur. Medications, through overdoses and adverse drug effects, are the source pharmacists deal with most.", "s": "tox:10", "r": null}, {"t": "The category is set by the setting in which a person meets the substance, so the same chemical can belong to different categories for different people. A chemotherapy drug is a medication for the patient. For the pharmacist who mixes it in a hood with the wrong airflow, it is an occupational exposure, because the exposure happens at work. Emergency room staff caring for a patient splattered with an organophosphate face an occupational exposure for the same reason, and radiation is another healthcare example. So healthcare workers are not free of occupational exposure.", "s": "tox:10", "r": null}, {"t": "Two more points separate right answers from wrong ones. A needle stick is a pathogen exposure, and this course does not treat pathogens as a toxicology source. She said, Remember, in this course we're not usually thinking about exposure to pathogens like needle sticks. Venoms are hard to attribute because they are complex mixtures, so it is not always known which chemicals in a venom cause the pain, the swelling and the death.", "s": "tox:10", "r": null}, {"t": "The same principle organizes the assigned reading on drugs in water. In that reading, the sources are human waste, medications that are flushed or improperly discarded, livestock, and treatment plants that do not remove all the drugs.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz"}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-4": {"title": "Route, duration, repetition and frequency of exposure", "segs": [{"t": "Section four. Exposure. The second principle asks how a person or animal is exposed. Four factors change the toxic response to the same substance: the route, how long the exposure lasts, whether it happens once or repeatedly, and, for repeated exposures, how often.", "s": "tox:11", "r": null}, {"t": "There are two route rankings, and they answer different questions. By how often people are exposed, the order is oral, then inhalation, then topical, then parenteral. By how effectively the route delivers a toxic agent, the order is intravenous, then inhalation, then oral, then dermal. So oral is the most common route, but intravenous is the most effective. In healthcare, parenteral exposure is much more common than in an occupational setting, through medications given by injection and through drug abuse.", "s": "tox:12", "r": null}, {"t": "The route can reduce or enhance toxicity because it changes metabolism. An oral dose goes to the liver before it reaches the rest of the body, and an intravenous dose does not. So if the liver turns a compound into something less toxic, equal doses make the oral route the less dangerous one. Formulation matters too. A handful of immediate-release beta blockers or calcium channel blockers is absorbed quickly. Extended-release tablets can clump together in the stomach and then release the drug all at once. Asked which handful is worse, she said, it depends. The vehicle matters as well: an oily vehicle and an aqueous vehicle for the same drug can change its toxicity when it is given inappropriately.", "s": "tox:12", "r": null}, {"t": "Duration has four terms from animal studies. Acute is less than twenty four hours. Subacute is less than one month. Subchronic is one to three months. Chronic is more than three months, up to years. A common wrong answer gives one to three months for subacute; that range is subchronic. In animal studies the duration is set by the design. People often do not know they are being exposed, or for how long, such as a funny smell at work that started days or months ago. Toxicologists still sort human exposures by the same four categories, to relate the response to the duration.", "s": "tox:13", "r": null}, {"t": "The single versus repeated slide makes three points. First, one exposure may act differently from repeated exposure. One walk into a workplace leaking benzene gives a headache and sleepiness, but chronic low-dose exposure causes leukemia, because benzene is a carcinogen. Second, a fast-absorbing compound likely gives an immediate effect, but may also give a delayed one. Diethylstilbestrol, D E S, a strong estrogen given in the nineteen fifties to women at risk of miscarriage, gave the mothers some nausea. Their daughters, exposed in utero, developed a particular vaginal cancer in their twenties. Third, chronic exposure may produce an immediate effect after each exposure: if the benzene leak is never fixed, the worker gets the headache every time.", "s": "tox:14", "r": null}, {"t": "Frequency is shown with an example from the assigned textbook chapter, Casarett and Doull chapter two. Three compounds are given either as one large dose or as repeated doses, each one quarter of the single dose, so four doses add up to the single dose. When the next dose arrives before the last one is cleared, the concentration climbs. Compound A has the longest half-life and reaches the toxic zone, a blood concentration above two, by the third dose. B is in between and gets there at about the fourth dose, certainly by the fifth. C has the shortest half-life, is cleared between doses, and never reaches the toxic zone. For a medication, the dosing interval is stretched until the drug behaves like C.", "s": "tox:15", "r": "casarett-ch2"}, {"t": "C can still cause harm. Staying below the toxic blood concentration protects only against the effect on that graph. A patient can be allergic to C, and a small amount is enough. C can reach a different target site, it can act together with other drugs to give additive adverse effects, and an injection under or into the skin can damage the injection site. None of these depends on half-life. So her practice quiz marks true the statement that a compound cleared before the next exposure may still result in chronic adverse effects.", "s": "tox:15", "r": null}], "mins": 5}, "guide3-5": {"title": "Bioactivation, detoxification and acetaminophen", "segs": [{"t": "Section five. Metabolism, bioactivation and acetaminophen. Everything absorbed into the body is distributed, usually metabolized, and excreted. In toxicology, metabolism gets the most attention, because it can turn a compound into something more harmful than what the person was exposed to. Bioactivation, also called toxication or metabolic activation, is metabolism that makes something more noxious than the original compound. Detoxification is metabolism to something less harmful and easier to eliminate. She said, I use the word bioactivation to talk about when metabolism occurs to create something that's more noxious than what you were originally exposed to.", "s": "tox:17", "r": null}, {"t": "Acetaminophen is her example. At the usual daily dose, two phase two enzyme families, the glucuronosyltransferases and the sulfotransferases, turn it into a glucuronide or a sulfate. That is detoxification. When the dose is more than they can handle, the rest goes to a phase one enzyme, cytochrome P four fifty two E one, which makes N A P Q I, a liver toxin. That is bioactivation. Glutathione S-transferases can then conjugate N A P Q I with glutathione, which is detoxification again. So an overdose damages the liver through a metabolite, not through the parent drug. The daily ceiling on the F D A labeling is four grams in twenty four hours.", "s": "tox:18", "r": null}, {"t": "A person can be injured at or below four grams for two separate reasons. The first is weak phase two enzymes. A genetic variation that lowers glucuronosyltransferase or sulfotransferase activity means part of a normal dose is pushed through two E one to make N A P Q I. She advises older parents not to take four grams in twenty four hours, because their phase two enzymes may be fewer. The second is induction, meaning the body makes more of an enzyme. Alcohol induces two E one, so a normal dose makes more N A P Q I. Her husband's example was three days of baseball with beers each evening, then five hundred milligram Tylenol tablets on Sunday, taken and forgotten until he neared four grams.", "s": "tox:18", "r": null}, {"t": "The two reasons can add together. Induced two E one plus low-activity phase two enzymes could really set up a person for liver toxicity by just taking the allowed dose of acetaminophen. They are still separate reasons: alcohol works by induction, not by lowering the phase two enzymes, and the low phase two activity is the genetic reason. Two E one is a phase one enzyme. When she says G S Ts she means the glucuronosyltransferases, and S Ts are the sulfotransferases. The glutathione S-transferases, which conjugate N A P Q I with glutathione, are a different family.", "s": null, "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-6": {"title": "The dose makes the poison, and the L D fifty", "segs": [{"t": "Section six. Dose and the L D fifty. The dose makes the poison, a saying attributed to Paracelsus, means any substance can harm at a high enough dose. That is why medications are poisons too. The second half of the idea is that the harmful dose differs enormously from one compound to another. Toxicologists compare compounds on that scale with the L D fifty, the lethal dose that kills fifty percent of the animals given the compound. It is not half the lethal dose. The values on her table come from animal exposure studies in rats.", "s": "tox:19", "r": "casarett-ch2"}, {"t": "Ethyl alcohol sits at the top of the table, with an L D fifty of ten thousand milligrams per kilogram. Botulinum toxin sits at the bottom, at one hundred-thousandth of a milligram per kilogram. The two differ by a factor of a billion. A smaller L D fifty means a smaller dose kills half the animals, so the compound is more lethal. A higher L D fifty means the compound is less acutely lethal, not more toxic. A large L D fifty is still not the same as safe, because alcohol poisoning still kills at the right dose. She said, So the dose makes the poison, but you have to know what compound you're talking about too.", "s": "tox:19", "r": null}, {"t": "Botulinum toxin is a toxin in the sense of section one, because a living organism makes it. The botulinum bacteria do not need oxygen, so they can grow sealed inside a can of a low-acid vegetable such as beans, peas or corn; tomatoes are usually fine. As they multiply, they make the toxin and build up pressure, so the can squirts when opened. What kills is the toxin, not the bacteria, and in her words cooking does not break the toxin down. She said, If the can squirts, throw it away, don't cook with it.", "s": null, "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-7": {"title": "Dose-response curves, U-shapes, threshold and cumulative dose", "segs": [{"t": "Section seven. Dose and response. The way dose-response data are graphed decides what can be read from them. An individual dose-response curve shows how each animal's response changes with its dose. In her example, rodents in dose groups were given an organophosphate, and percent inhibition of two enzymes, carboxylesterase and cholinesterase, was plotted against dose. Because each response is plotted separately, the graph shows that many chemicals have more than one effect, because they have multiple targets. She said, we could not see this if we weren't graphing it as an individual dose response curve.", "s": "tox:20", "r": null}, {"t": "A population dose-response curve groups the responses and shows how many animals respond at each dose, as mortality frequency and cumulative mortality. Two values are read from it. The L D fifty is where the cumulative curve crosses fifty percent, about one hundred milligrams per kilogram on her figure. The threshold dose is the lowest dose with a response, about ten milligrams per kilogram. She does not use the probit panel of this figure.", "s": "tox:21", "r": null}, {"t": "Two U-shaped curves look alike for different reasons. Iron is an essential substance. With too little, the blood cannot carry oxygen, and the adverse response can be death. Too much is an iron overdose, which can also kill. In the middle is a region of homeostasis. Red wine shows hormesis. No wine gives no benefit, a glass or a glass and a half may be protective, and more gives an adverse effect. Because wine is not essential, the low end is not a deficiency, and there is no region of homeostasis.", "s": "tox:22", "r": null}, {"t": "Two curve shapes describe what happens at low doses. A sigmoid curve has a threshold: below some dose there is no effect, therapeutic or poisonous. That is probably true for medications and most poisons. A linear curve has no threshold: from zero dose, the first molecule could cause a problem. That was long the theory for carcinogens, because one molecule could possibly damage D N A and start a cancer. So a substance is not assumed to have a harmless dose range.", "s": "tox:23", "r": null}, {"t": "The last idea is cumulative dose. Animals in dose groups were fed two acetylaminofluorene, two A A F, every day, and it causes liver and bladder tumors. One set was sacrificed at twenty four months and another at thirty three months. The bladder tumor threshold fell from about seventy five parts per million to about forty five. She said, use the keyword cumulative. The older animals had received a higher total dose, so a lower daily dose was enough. At thirty three months even the zero-dose animals had liver tumors, because cancer is usually a disease of the aged, so this species is a poor choice for that endpoint. She calls the animals hamsters; the textbook chapter says mice.", "s": "tox:24", "r": "casarett-ch2"}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-8": {"title": "Therapeutic index, margin of safety and margin of exposure", "segs": [{"t": "Section eight. Three indices on one slide. Three numbers are calculated from dose-response curves. Each is a ratio of a dose that harms, or the highest dose with no observed harm, to a dose that helps, or the dose people actually receive. So a larger number means more room before harm.", "s": "tox:27", "r": null}, {"t": "The therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose, usually T D fifty over E D fifty. It is for single doses and gives no information about slope. The margin of safety is T D one over E D ninety nine. It is also for single doses, and because it compares the low end of the toxic curve with the high end of the effective curve, it does give slope information. Her practice quiz asked which value is toxic dose over therapeutic dose; the answer is the therapeutic index, not the margin of safety.", "s": "tox:27", "r": null}, {"t": "The margin of exposure is the N O A E L over the exposure. It is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds. The N O A E L is the no observed adverse effect level, the highest dose in the animal studies at which no adverse effect was observed.", "s": "tox:27", "r": null}], "mins": 1}, "guide3-l14-1": {"title": "Potency, the three dose curves, and the three indices", "segs": [{"t": "Section nine. Potency and the values built from dose-response curves. These curves are used to compare compounds, and to compare the different effects of one compound. If compound A gives the measured response at a lower dose than compound B, A is more potent. Compounds C and D are the trap. C responds at lower doses and then levels off, while D keeps rising and crosses C. So C is more potent at low doses, and past the crossing, D is more potent. She said, you can't say that C is always more potent than D. You can in the lower doses.", "s": "tox:25", "r": "casarett-ch2"}, {"t": "Why would C level off? She accepted several answers, all of which could be true. The receptors C binds could be saturated. The metabolism that turns C into its active form could saturate, or an uptake transporter in the gut, such as P glycoprotein. C could be a partial agonist. Target, tissue, metabolism, distribution and absorption can each change potency with dose. This is also why a study needs a wide range of doses. Had it stopped before the curves crossed, nobody would see the potency change places.", "s": "tox:25", "r": null}, {"t": "For one compound, as the dose rises, the effective dose comes first, then the toxic dose, then the lethal dose, and the three curves are not parallel. Differences in absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion are one reason. The more important one is the fifth principle. The mechanism by which the drug is effective, toxic and lethal is different for each outcome, and different mechanisms give different slopes.", "s": "tox:26", "r": null}, {"t": "Her therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose, usually the T D fifty over the E D fifty. Another percentage works, as long as top and bottom use the same one. It tells how much dosing error can be tolerated. A wide index means the toxic dose is far above the therapeutic dose, so there is less chance of toxicity. A narrow index means the toxic dose is not much higher.", "s": "tox:27", "r": null}, {"t": "The therapeutic index compares the two curves only at their fifty percent points. Two drugs can share the same T D fifty and E D fifty while one has a much flatter toxic curve. The margin of safety, the T D one over the E D ninety nine, compares the toxic dose for the most sensitive one percent with the dose that works in ninety nine percent. Because it uses those different points, a flatter or steeper curve changes it. She said, because we use a different percentage, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not.", "s": "tox:27", "r": null}, {"t": "The margin of exposure is for repeated exposure to compounds that are not medications, such as a pollutant. A pollutant has no therapeutic dose, so there is nothing to put in a therapeutic index. Instead, the highest dose without an observed adverse effect in animal studies is divided by the estimated human exposure. At a site with several contaminants, the compound with the smallest margin of exposure is the most dangerous, so it is the one to clean up first. The value of all three numbers is the comparison between compounds.", "s": "tox:27", "r": null}], "mins": 4}, "guide3-l14-2": {"title": "Target and mechanism of action", "segs": [{"t": "Section ten. Target and mechanism of action. The fifth principle asks where in the body a compound acts and how it causes harm. The target can be a specific organ or a more general physiological endpoint. A compound can cause problems at more than one place, and not every place it hits may be known yet. If a compound damages the kidney and the liver, it likely damages them in different ways, because each target has its own mechanism.", "s": "tox:28", "r": null}, {"t": "The target organ may not be the site of highest concentration, because tissues differ in sensitivity. A compound can be most concentrated in muscle and cause no harm there, yet damage a more sensitive kidney at a lower concentration. She said, where it's having an adverse effect may not be where the highest concentration is, which is probably a little counterintuitive. Sensitivity still needs dose. The kidney has its own dose-response curve, so even a very sensitive organ is harmed only if the concentration reaching it is high enough.", "s": "tox:28", "r": null}, {"t": "The mechanism at each target may be different. That is why the effective, toxic and lethal curves of one drug are not parallel, and why many adverse effects on a drug's list have nothing to do with its therapeutic action. There are two kinds of side effect. Some are the therapy going too far at the intended target, such as a diuretic that removes too much fluid and leaves the patient needing hydration. Others come from a different target altogether. Aminoglycoside ototoxicity is her classic example of an adverse effect whose target is very different from the therapeutic one.", "s": "tox:28", "r": null}, {"t": "Her true or false items turn on the word always. The statement that a medication's concentration is always highest at its target site is false. The statement that a drug's mechanism of toxicity is always the same as its mechanism of efficacy is false. And a highly sensitive organ is not always damaged, because the dose at the target still decides.", "s": "tox:28", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l14-3": {"title": "Drugs in our water, the Rodriguez-Mozaz and Weinberg reading", "segs": [{"t": "Section eleven. The reading by Rodriguez-Mozaz and Weinberg on drugs in our water. The article reports on an environmental health summit where government agencies, academics, the pharmaceutical industry and other scientists met for the first time about drugs found in surface water, groundwater and drinking water. The assigned part is a real case of the five principles, and her discussion guide compares each principle for humans and for aquatic wildlife. Her conclusion was that people are probably fine, wildlife is the bigger concern, and antibiotic resistance in the environment is a real problem.", "s": "tox:9", "r": "rodriguez-mozaz"}, {"t": "The principles are not labeled in the article; she said they were buried in it, and her discussion guide sorts them out. The assigned part has four headings: Sources of pharmaceuticals in water, Exposure of humans and wildlife, Effects on wildlife, and Human health. The feminization findings she used for wildlife dose and target are under Effects on wildlife. The last heading, Best management practices, is not assigned.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Source is the same for humans and wildlife. Drugs reach the water by flushing, by animal waste from feedlots and farms, and by human waste. People take medications every day and excrete the unchanged drug and its metabolites, and wastewater treatment plants do not remove them all before the water returns to streams, rivers and lakes. The article itself names wastewater treatment plants as the main points where drugs are discharged into surface water, because their treatment steps were not designed to remove pharmaceuticals. Flushing and animal waste can be reduced by changing practices. Human waste cannot, because people will keep taking medications. So in her key the greatest source is consumption of medications by humans and the resulting waste streams, not flushing unused medications, not landfills and not manufacturing.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Exposure is where humans and wildlife differ. People are exposed by drinking water, eating fish, and skin contact while bathing or swimming, at about parts per trillion. At that level, even a well-hydrated person does not come close to a normal therapeutic daily dose. She said, so I think for humans we can be OK. Doesn't mean we should ignore our sensitive populations. Those are the fetus, children, the elderly and people with underlying disease. Aquatic animals drink the water, eat food from it, absorb it through their skin, and fish breathe it, constantly and for their entire lives. That is why her key calls wildlife exposure the more significant one, not because the concentrations are known to be higher.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz", "tb": 0}, {"t": "For humans, some data on absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion exist from drug development, because drugs were tested in rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and sometimes pigs. Nobody tests drugs in sea otters, orca whales, fish or mollusks, so for wildlife there are very little if any data. The dose is not known exactly for either group, because the values often were not measured, were not detected, or were modeled. For humans it is probably below the therapeutic dose. For wildlife the doses that cause harm are unknown, but given the nature of their exposure, the dose is likely high enough to cause effects. For both, the target is unknown, including how multiple exposures act together.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Some effects are seen. Estrogen is higher off the shorelines of big cities with many young women, possibly because many take oral contraceptives. There, populations of frogs, some fish and alligators shift from about half male and half female to more female. Frogs absorb a lot through their skin and are a sentinel species, an early warning that something is wrong. Is wildlife being harmed? She accepted both yes and we don't know, because there is evidence for some species, while most others, such as orca whales, are too hard to study. Could warming water cause the shift instead of estrogen? Studies that exposed these species to estrogenic compounds do produce the shift, so the link to estrogen is plausible, while warming water needs its own studies. She said it could be both.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Bacteria in water and sediments that live with released antibiotics are becoming resistant, and a resistant infection, for example through a cut at the beach, may not be treatable. The authors' own conclusion was that more data are needed to determine whether pharmaceuticals in water are adversely affecting human health. They did not conclude that drugs in water are harming people, or that wildlife is unaffected. So a patient afraid of drugs in the water supply can be told the dose is far below a therapeutic dose, with open questions for sensitive populations and for any future rise in concentrations.", "s": null, "r": "rodriguez-mozaz", "tb": 0}], "mins": 5}, "guide3-l14-4": {"title": "General concepts of undesired effects", "segs": [{"t": "Section twelve. General concepts of undesired effects, ways to describe any toxic effect before an organ is named. The first pair is immediate versus delayed. Immediate effects occur rapidly after a single administration. Delayed effects occur after some time has passed. Diethylstilbestrol, called D E S, is delayed by years, with vaginal cancer in the daughters as young adults. Some organophosphates, such as triorthocresylphosphate, are delayed by days, causing neuropathy by affecting other enzymes in the nerves. Immediate effects can also occur with each dose in a chronic exposure, as with the benzene worker.", "s": "resp:4", "r": null}, {"t": "Whether an injury is reversible depends on the ability of the tissue to regenerate. Liver cells can divide and replace damaged tissue, so a mild liver injury can heal, though severe chronic injury may not. The differentiated cells of the central nervous system cannot divide, so most nerve injuries are irreversible. Cancer is irreversible even when treated, because surgery, radiation or chemotherapy kill the cancerous cells, but the cells never turn back into normal ones. A birth defect is irreversible because the limb or organ formed wrongly during development.", "s": "resp:5", "r": null}, {"t": "Local effects occur at the site of first contact. Systemic effects require absorption at the site of entry and distribution to the site of action. She said, most toxins, they cause systemic effects unless they are highly reactive. Highly reactive compounds such as chlorine gas or acid react where they first touch, in the lungs, eyes and nasal mucosa, or on the skin. Tetraethyl lead does both. A couple of drops on a toxicologist's glove burned her hand, were absorbed through the burned skin, reached her central nervous system and killed her days later.", "s": "resp:6", "r": null}, {"t": "An acid burn can cause an indirect systemic effect. A large acid burn behaves like a thermal burn. The skin cannot hold fluid in, blood volume is disturbed, and serious kidney problems can follow as the body fights to keep fluid in. The acid itself never had to be absorbed. So a burned patient is assessed systemically, not only at the skin.", "s": "resp:6", "r": null}, {"t": "People are exposed to many compounds at once, through the workplace, drugs, diet and hobbies. One compound can change the absorption, protein binding, biotransformation or excretion of another. The combined response can be additive, synergistic, potentiation or antagonism. Each compound may have a known target and effect, but these interactions are hard to measure, so a risk assessor at a site with many contaminants cannot easily put one number on the risk.", "s": "resp:7", "r": null}, {"t": "Tolerance is decreased responsiveness to a toxic effect resulting from prior exposure to the chemical or a structurally related compound. There are two mechanisms. Dispositional tolerance means less compound reaches the site. Carbon tetrachloride is metabolized to something more harmful, but repeated exposure inhibits those enzymes, so less of the harmful metabolite forms. Cadmium induces the metal-carrying protein that binds it, which helps the kidneys eliminate it. So carbon tetrachloride inhibits, and cadmium induces. The second mechanism is reduced responsiveness, a pharmacodynamic change. The receptors are down-regulated, so the same dose gives less effect.", "s": "resp:8", "r": null}, {"t": "Variations in effect come in three kinds. Selective toxicity means lethal to one species but not another. Species differences means both species respond, but differently in kind or amount, which limits extrapolation between species. Arsenic in drinking water causes skin cancer on the palms of the hands and feet in humans, but no animal tested reproduces it. Had only animals been tested, arsenic in water would have been judged safe for humans. Individual differences within one species come from genetics, which pharmacogenomics studies.", "s": "resp:9", "r": null}], "mins": 4}, "guide3-l14-5": {"title": "On-target and off-target endpoints", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirteen. On-target and off-target toxic endpoints. Toxic endpoints are the results of an exposure. Besides on-target and off-target, they may be non-organ specific, organ specific, idiosyncratic, meaning the cause is unknown, or immunologic, mainly allergic reactions. The on-target and off-target labels answer one question. Did the drug or its metabolite bind the receptor it was designed for? The tissue where that happens does not change the label.", "s": "resp:11", "r": null}, {"t": "On-target in the intended tissue usually means too much drug at the receptor. She said, most of the time, if you're having an on target adverse effect in the right tissue, your dose is too high. That can come from a deliberate or accidental dosing error, from a change in the drug's pharmacokinetics, such as a diseased liver that cannot clear a normal dose, or from a change in receptor number.", "s": "resp:13", "r": null}, {"t": "On-target in the wrong tissue means the drug reaches its intended receptor where it should not act. Diphenhydramine, Benadryl, blocks allergic symptoms through histamine receptors in the periphery and causes drowsiness through the same histamine receptors in the brain. That drowsiness is on-target, not off-target. Newer antihistamines, cetirizine and fexofenadine, were modified so they do not cross the blood-brain barrier, so they do not cause drowsiness.", "s": "resp:13", "r": null}, {"t": "Off-target means the drug binds a receptor it was not designed for, in either tissue. Terfenadine, Seldane, gave the desired peripheral H one antagonism but also inhibited cardiac potassium channels, causing arrhythmias, and it was taken off the market. Racemic thalidomide contained the R enantiomer, an effective anti-nausea agent, and the S enantiomer, a potent teratogen. Propranolol lowers heart rate through beta one receptors in the heart but worsens asthma through beta two receptors in airway smooth muscle. An enantiomer and a receptor subtype both count as unintended receptors.", "s": "resp:14", "r": null}, {"t": "Her quiz defines an off-target adverse effect as one that occurs when a drug interacts with a receptor that is not its therapeutic target. The wrong choices describe on-target effects, either a dose too high at the intended receptor, or the intended receptor in the wrong location. Off-target binding explains side effects on a drug's list that have nothing to do with its mechanism of action. One more trap is receptor number. Down-regulation means fewer receptors and a smaller response, not a larger one.", "s": "resp:12", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l14-6": {"title": "Non-organ-specific endpoints", "segs": [{"t": "Section fourteen. Non-organ-specific endpoints. An endpoint is non-organ specific when the damage arises by the same mechanism and looks the same whichever organ it shows up in. A compound may favor one organ, but the endpoint is classified by its pathology, not its location. Her four are fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis. They contrast with the organ-specific endpoints of the lungs, nervous system, liver and kidney.", "s": "resp:11", "r": null}, {"t": "In fibrosis, excess collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins are laid down, the tissue becomes stiff, and the organ loses function. Lungs with paraquat fibrosis can no longer inhale and exhale. Pulmonary fibrosis forms in the alveolar interstitium, from amiodarone, a cardiac drug, bleomycin, a chemotherapy drug, and paraquat, an herbicide. Liver fibrosis is cirrhosis, from ethanol, and amiodarone causes it too. She said, if you look at the chemistry of that fibrotic tissue, it's the same. So fibrosis is non-organ specific even though amiodarone and bleomycin hit the lung.", "s": "resp:15", "r": null}, {"t": "Genotoxicity is damage to D N A in general, in somatic or germ cells. Mutagenicity is a transmissible change. A mutation in germ cells can pass to offspring, which makes the agent a mutagen, while damage in a somatic cell stays with the exposed person. So the two words are not synonyms. The results of genotoxicity can be cancer, birth defects, and genetically based disorders.", "s": "resp:16", "r": null}, {"t": "Cancer may have a long latency between exposure to a carcinogen and the disease. The multistage model of initiation, promotion and progression she summarized in one line. It takes more than one mutation to end up with cancer. Cancer rates do differ by organ, but rates are not the basis of the classification. The same cellular mechanism can cause cancers in different organs, and some lung cancers carry the same mutation as some colon cancers. So the wrong answers are that cancer is not understood well enough, or that cancers in different organs share nothing in their pathology.", "s": "resp:17", "r": null}, {"t": "Teratogenesis is the induction of birth defects. The fetus is exposed through the mother, so its exposure depends on her absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, and on whether the compound crosses the placenta. Time of exposure decides the type of defect. Retinoids given to pregnant hamsters on different gestation days caused small eyes, spina bifida, cleft palate, forelimb shortening and effects on the tail. So the statement that each teratogen causes only one kind of birth defect is false.", "s": "resp:21", "r": null}, {"t": "The reverse also holds. Several compounds cause the same palate deformity, each with its own peak gestation day. Methanol peaks on day seven, T C D D on day eleven, two, four, five trichloroacetic acid on day twelve, and dexamethasone on day thirteen. So a defect alone does not identify the compound. Dose matters as well. Her one sentence was that the mother has to get enough of the compound for it to pass into the baby.", "s": "resp:22", "r": null}, {"t": "The Food and Drug Administration used to rank drugs by how likely they were to cause birth defects. Her quiz keys three contents of the newer Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rules, from two thousand fourteen. They are effects on female and male reproductive potential, risks to the baby during breastfeeding, and risks to the baby during pregnancy. Information about how a medication will affect a child if given to the child is not one of them.", "s": "resp:24", "r": null}], "mins": 4}, "guide3-l15-1": {"title": "Pulmonary toxicity", "segs": [{"t": "Section fifteen. Pulmonary toxicity. For each organ she asks why the whole organ is susceptible and why certain parts of it are. The lungs are exposed because we breathe all the time, and they carry an oxidative burden. We breathe in oxygen and pollutants carrying reactive oxygen species, such as sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides. White blood cells that come in to fight pathogens or clean up damaged tissue release more. The result damages the fats and proteins of lung cells.", "s": "resp:25", "r": null}, {"t": "For a gas, the property that matters is water solubility, not size, because it decides how far down the gas goes. She said very hydrophilic gases go far down into the lungs and hydrophobic gases do not. For particles, size, shape and breathing pattern decide where they deposit, and heavier breathing, jogging rather than walking, brings in more. Clearance by coughing and by the cilia of the upper airway decides how long particles stay. Because deposition depends on what was inhaled, the site of lung damage points back to the likely cause.", "s": "resp:25", "r": null}, {"t": "Very small particles are so light they are breathed back out. Large particles come in fast, cannot turn the curve into the next branch, and impact the upper airway where the bronchioles bifurcate. Medium particles are heavy enough not to be breathed out and small enough to go around the curves, so in the pause at the end of a breath in, they settle in the alveolar regions. She said, the medium sized particles are the ones that are the most concerning. Long, thin, sharp asbestos fibers embed at the upper bifurcations, and some move through the tissue and cause mesothelioma on the outer lining of the lungs.", "s": "resp:25", "r": null}, {"t": "Acute responses are airway reactivity, where bronchoconstriction narrows the airway and causes wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and dyspnea, and pulmonary edema, which thickens the alveolar-capillary barrier. Chronic responses are fibrosis, emphysema, where the lungs become too plastic and trap air, and asthma. Asthma is on her chronic list even though attacks are sudden. Fluorine gas, chlorine gas and the other halogens give acute responses, because they are very caustic with the water in the lungs.", "s": "resp:26", "r": null}, {"t": "Pulmonary toxicity does not require inhalation. Bleomycin and cyclophosphamide, chemotherapy drugs given intravenously, and the herbicides monocrotaline and paraquat reach the lungs through the blood and cause pulmonary fibrosis. She said, we give them by I V, but they're known to cause pulmonary toxicity. This is pulmonary toxicity by location, while fibrosis itself stays non-organ specific by its mechanism.", "s": "resp:26", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l15-10": {"title": "Evidence-based guidelines for overdoses", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty-four. Evidence-based guidelines for overdoses. For a short list of compounds, expert panels have written Evidence-Based Consensus Guidelines for Out-of-Hospital Management. They cover what to do when someone is found after an overdose, before any hospital is involved, and who must be sent in. Medications on the list include acetaminophen, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, iron, salicylate and tricyclic antidepressants. Three are not medications. Elemental mercury, ethylene glycol, and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides. Ethylene glycol is on the list even though it is not a drug.", "s": "treat:30", "r": null}, {"t": "Overdoses cannot be studied in clinical trials, because a trial of deliberate overdose would not be ethical. Animals are used very rarely, only to find out whether a treatment really works. Instead, clinicians, pharmacists and physicians write up the overdoses they treat and publish them as case reports or case series. The experts who wrote the guidelines pulled all of these and evaluated every one.", "s": "treat:31", "r": null}, {"t": "She said, they rank the data that they have to create these documents, and they rank the strength of the recommendations they make in these documents. That grading is what makes them evidence based, without any trial. In the guidelines' grading table, case reports and case series are level four evidence, which goes with grade C, and expert opinion is level five, which goes with grade D. In the calcium channel blocker guideline, all seven recommendations are grade D. Both documents were published in two thousand five in Clinical Toxicology and are still followed.", "s": "treat:31", "r": "aapcc-ccb"}, {"t": "The guidelines were written for poison center staff taking calls about patients still at home. So they are about triage, who must go to the emergency department and who can stay home. Each document answers the same questions. There is no vomiting at home, and activated charcoal is for the hospital, with transport not delayed to give it. For a patient who is asymptomatic when found, there is a time window, by formulation, inside which the patient goes to the hospital. After it, home care with poison center follow-up is reasonable. They also cover symptomatic patients, ambulance transport, follow-up, suspected self-harm, and dose thresholds for referral. In each document, the seven graded recommendations come near the end, and an appendix turns them into a triage algorithm.", "s": "treat:31", "r": "aapcc-bb"}, {"t": "She named two contrasts between the beta blocker and calcium channel blocker documents. One is the drug class itself, even though both are cardiac drugs. The other is immediate-release versus extended-release tablets. Extended-release tablets get different windows because they can form bezoars that break apart at unpredictable times, so the time of absorption cannot be known.", "s": "treat:31", "r": "aapcc-bb"}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l15-2": {"title": "Neurotoxicity", "segs": [{"t": "Section sixteen. Neurotoxicity. The nervous system has one strong protection, the blood-brain barrier, and four features that make it vulnerable. In a systemic capillary, the wall has openings and the surrounding pericytes are not tightly joined, so solutes move into the tissue between cells. In a brain capillary, the cells of the vessel touch tightly at tight junctions and are surrounded by tightly connected glial cells. A compound must pass through the endothelial cell membranes, so lipophilicity decides entry, and non-lipophilic and larger compounds are kept out. Transporters move some compounds back into the blood.", "s": "resp:27", "r": null}, {"t": "The barrier has two weak points. It is not continuous, and in some parts of the brain it is looser. And it is immature at birth. She said, that blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth for several months to years. Some drugs that stay out of adult brains crossed into very young children's brains and harmed them, before this was known.", "s": "resp:27", "r": null}, {"t": "Neurons depend on aerobic metabolism and have very high energy needs, so any exposure that lowers oxygen or energy supply affects the nervous system. Its cells are often very long, such as peripheral nerves running from the spinal cord down the limbs. Neurons must make a large cell volume and move nutrients and waste up and down the axon, which adds to the energy demand.", "s": "resp:28", "r": null}, {"t": "No other organ has myelin. Schwann cells in the periphery and oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system wrap the axon in many lipid layers and speed conduction. When myelin is damaged, transmission slows, with symptoms such as tingling of the hands and feet. The nervous system is also the only part of the body that depends on neurotransmission, meaning release, crossing the cleft, binding the postsynaptic cell and reuptake, and disturbing any step gives symptoms.", "s": "resp:29", "r": null}, {"t": "A neurotoxin usually causes one discrete pattern of injury, and the patterns look different under a microscope. In neuronopathy the entire neuron dies. In axonopathy only the axon degenerates, as if cut chemically, while the cell body and myelin are spared. In myelinopathy the myelinating cells are damaged, and remyelination happens slowly, much less effectively in the central nervous system. Her example was metronidazole, which made her hands and feet tingle on a second, higher-dose course. In neurotransmission toxicity, as with organophosphate pesticides, the cell looks normal and the injury is recognized from the loss of function. So in her tingling story, the pattern is myelinopathy, not axonopathy.", "s": "resp:31", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l15-3": {"title": "Hepatotoxicity and the kinds of liver injury", "segs": [{"t": "Section seventeen. Hepatotoxicity, why the liver is susceptible, and the kinds of liver injury. Blood enters each lobe of the liver at its edges through the hepatic artery and the portal vein. It trickles along the cords of hepatocytes and drains out through the central vein. Bile ducts begin at the edges of the lobe. Anything swallowed travels in the portal circulation to the liver first, and that is the basis of the first-pass effect.", "s": "resp:32", "r": null}, {"t": "Her poll asked why the liver is so susceptible to drug toxicity. Four reasons are correct. It takes up and concentrates compounds from the blood. She said, its job is to take up and concentrate stuff out of the blood. It receives a large volume of blood through the portal circulation, so everything swallowed reaches it first. It does a lot of metabolism, so it can bioactivate compounds to hepatotoxic metabolites right where they do harm. And it has its own inflammatory cells, the Kupffer cells. The one wrong option was that it is a large organ. Size has nothing to do with it.", "s": "resp:33", "r": null}, {"t": "Kupffer cells are there to help. They clean up pathogens and dead or damaged cells. But when the liver tissue itself is damaged, their cleanup releases inflammatory cytokines, which call in more immune cells, and reactive oxygen species. So the inflammation keeps going and adds to the injury. The liver also tends to have idiosyncratic reactions, because it does most of the metabolism. A person with a rare genetic variant may form a hepatotoxic metabolite that most people do not. Once the genotype is known, the reaction is explained. Kidneys, lungs and the central nervous system do not show this pattern much.", "s": "resp:33", "r": null}, {"t": "The kinds of liver injury come from a Klaassen table. She requires the kinds of injury and the ability to explain them, not the drug lists. Hepatocyte death is death of the cells that do much of the metabolism, as with acetaminophen. Fatty liver, or steatosis, is fat building up in the liver. Canalicular cholestasis is blocked bile flow. The bile duct narrows because the cells lining it are damaged and inflamed. That is not the same as hepatocyte death. The table also lists bile duct damage, sinusoidal endothelial damage, inflammation, regeneration, fibrosis and cirrhosis, and liver cancers.", "s": "resp:34", "r": null}, {"t": "Most hepatotoxins cause one discrete kind of injury. Aflatoxin, made by molds on grains, causes only liver cancer. Ethanol is the exception. It appears under cell death, steatosis, inflammation, regeneration and fibrosis. She said, each compound is usually very discrete in the kind of hepatotoxicity it'll cause. Because of that, imaging and a liver biopsy that show the pattern of injury give a hint of what the patient was exposed to.", "s": "resp:34", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l15-4": {"title": "Liver zones, the oxygen gradient, and C Y P two E one", "segs": [{"t": "Section eighteen. Liver zones, the oxygen gradient, and C Y P two E one. Within the liver, a hepatotoxin often damages one zone and spares the others. The acinus view follows the blood from the vessels that bring it in, the hepatic artery and the portal vein, across to the central vein where it leaves. Zone one is closest to the inflow vessels. Zone three is closest to the central vein. Zone two lies between them.", "s": "resp:35", "r": null}, {"t": "The first reason for site-specific damage is the oxygen gradient. Blood enters zone one fairly well oxygenated. As it flows past the hepatocytes, they take oxygen out of it, so by the time it reaches the central vein it is relatively hypoxic. Zone one has the most oxygen and zone three the least. If the enzymes that bioactivate a compound need oxygen to work, the high oxygen in zone one helps them. The hepatotoxic metabolite is made there, and zone one is damaged. Relatively high oxygen content in zone one was the keyed answer to her poll. Reversing the gradient is a trap.", "s": "resp:35", "r": null}, {"t": "The second reason is where the enzymes are. Zone one has many phase two enzymes. Zone three has some P four fifties, and C Y P two E one is expressed more there than in zone one. In overdose, acetaminophen is bioactivated to N A P Q I by C Y P two E one. Because C Y P two E one is highest in zone three, N A P Q I is made in zone three, and a biopsy shows hepatocyte death in zone three. She said, most of our metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneously expressed across the entire liver.", "s": "resp:35", "r": null}, {"t": "Two other options on that poll are false. Homogenous expression of P four fifties across the zones is false, and C Y P two E one is the counterexample. Zone two having few Kupffer cells is also false. Kupffer cells move all over the liver, in every zone, so they cannot explain one zone being spared. The two reasons for site-specific liver damage are the oxygen gradient across the zones, and the uneven expression of metabolizing enzymes such as the C Y P four fifties.", "s": "resp:35", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l15-5": {"title": "Nephrotoxicity, N S A I Ds and P P Is", "segs": [{"t": "Section nineteen. Nephrotoxicity. The kidneys are susceptible for four reasons. They receive a large delivery of blood, especially blood that has passed through the liver carrying metabolites. Concentrating the urine also concentrates poisons in the tubule, and the tubular cells take compounds up into themselves. Compounds can be metabolized by the kidney cells, so toxicity in place is likely. And the kidney is sensitive to endogenous vasoconstrictors.", "s": "resp:37", "r": null}, {"t": "That last reason is unique to the kidney. Filtration depends on blood flow through the arterioles. Constricting the afferent or efferent arteriole reduces blood flow and so reduces G F R. She said, if you reduce that blood flow, you're going to reduce G F R. And because the kidney concentrates solutes in its tubules and its cells, renal cells are exposed to more of a compound than the blood level suggests.", "s": "resp:36", "r": null}, {"t": "The kinds of renal injury come from a Klaassen table. They are prerenal, vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity, endothelial injury, glomerulopathy, and interstitial nephritis. She requires the kinds, not the drug lists. N S A I Ds are the class she worries about most, and she named three injuries for them. Interstitial nephritis, vasoconstriction and glomerulopathy. She also told the class to add P P Is, such as omeprazole, under interstitial nephritis, even though they are not on the table. The over-the-counter label limits use to two weeks, because reflux that needs a P P I longer should be checked, and because these drugs can cause interstitial nephritis.", "s": "resp:38", "r": null}, {"t": "Injury is site specific. Not every nephrotoxin damages the whole kidney, and a quiz statement saying all nephrotoxins damage the entire kidney is false. The slide lists reasons such as differences in blood flow, transport and accumulation, and the balance of bioactivation and detoxification. Her most important reason is which enzymes are expressed in which part of the nephron. Tubular toxicity and crystalluria depend more on the chemistry of the drug.", "s": "resp:39", "r": null}, {"t": "She described her father's case. A geriatric patient, allergic to aspirin, was told to take over-the-counter omeprazole for reflux while his physical was rescheduled. Months later, routine blood work showed a high serum creatinine. The only change was the P P I. The literature described P P I induced interstitial nephritis, especially in patients with an aspirin allergy. She said, that allergic response is manifesting as interstitial nephritis in some patients, so it's not like an allergic response where you get like hives. He stopped the P P I for good. Kidney injury can be silent until a creatinine is measured.", "s": "resp:38", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l15-6": {"title": "Idiosyncratic and immunologic responses", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty. Idiosyncratic and immunologic responses. These two toxic endpoints fit neither the organ-specific nor the non-organ-specific group. The slide defines an idiosyncratic reaction as genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a chemical. The response is qualitatively similar to other people's, but extreme. Using the word idiosyncratic for every low-frequency reaction is not proper use of the term.", "s": "resp:40", "r": null}, {"t": "She said, the liver is the organ that is most likely to have an idiosyncratic reaction compared to other organs in the body. The reason is that the liver does most drug metabolism. A person with a rare genetic variant may metabolize a drug differently and form a toxic metabolite that others do not. Before pharmacogenomics this looked unexplainable. With the genotype known, it is an unusual but explained reaction.", "s": "resp:40", "r": null}, {"t": "The next slide is titled Allergic Responses. She asked the class to change it to immunologic responses, because allergy is only one of two kinds. In an immunologic reaction, the drug is recognized as foreign and the immune system responds to it. Small molecules, under six hundred daltons, bind body proteins and act as haptens, and the drug and protein complex triggers the response. Large drugs, the therapeutic peptides and proteins, activate the immune system directly. So small drugs can provoke an immune response too.", "s": "resp:41", "r": null}, {"t": "The two mechanisms are hypersensitivity, meaning type one, two, three or four allergic responses, and autoimmune reactions, where the immune system attacks the patient's own body. Autoimmune reactions are increasing as more biologicals reach the market. She said, oftentimes, those immune responses to those biological therapies are hard to predict, and they're often kind of bad. An immunologic reaction can also appear in one organ. Her father's P P I reaction appeared as interstitial nephritis.", "s": "resp:41", "r": null}, {"t": "She asked four poll questions on toxic responses. The first asked which statements about toxic effects are true. Two are true. A delayed response may be seen weeks to years after exposure. And immediate effects can occur with each dose in a chronic exposure, such as a workplace leak that causes a headache every day. Three are false: that tissue injury from a toxic exposure is always reversible, that toxins will not cause local effects, and that systemic effects do not require absorption. To become systemic, a compound has to be absorbed into the blood.", "s": "resp:42", "r": null}, {"t": "In the second poll, teratogenicity is the non-organ-specific response, because the other options each name an organ. The third poll asked why the liver is so susceptible, and every option was correct except that it is a large organ. The fourth asked for one reason for regional susceptibility across the liver, and the answer was the relatively high oxygen content in zone one.", "s": "resp:42", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l15-7": {"title": "How pharmacokinetics change in overdose", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty-one. How pharmacokinetics change in overdose. The parameters measured at therapeutic doses may not hold in an overdose. She said, so you got therapeutic kinetics, and then you got overdose kinetics for a lot of drugs. The parameters that change most are volume of distribution and clearance. A quiz statement that overdose will never affect the kinetics of a drug is false.", "s": "treat:4", "r": null}, {"t": "Some drugs have a large volume of distribution, over five liters per kilogram. Examples include antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids and verapamil. A large volume means most of the drug has left the blood and is sequestered in lipid, muscle or bone. Dialysis can only clear what is in the blood. After one run, drug moves back into the blood, and dialysis must wait for it. So a large volume makes a drug hard to dialyze, not easy. The DiPiro chapter gives the profile of a dialyzable drug. Volume of distribution under one liter per kilogram, protein binding under eighty percent, and molecular weight under one thousand daltons.", "s": "treat:4", "r": "dipiro-e9"}, {"t": "Absorption can change in two directions. Slowed tablet dissolution or gastric emptying delays absorption, so peak toxic effects come later than expected. Iron goes the other way. Oral iron is absorbed by active transport, which saturates, so normally only a limited amount gets in. In overdose, iron destroys the gut lining, so more is absorbed. She said, iron is very caustic, it actually blows apart your gut. Gut injury here increases absorption rather than reducing it.", "s": "treat:4", "r": null}, {"t": "Saturated liver metabolism means less first-pass effect. The excess passes through unmetabolized, so blood levels are higher than expected, not lower. Plasma protein binding capacity can also be exceeded. Past that capacity the extra drug stays free, and free drug carries the toxicity. This means proteins in the blood, not binding in tissue. Finally, high plasma concentrations may change elimination from first order to zero order, which lengthens the half-life.", "s": "treat:4", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l15-8": {"title": "How pharmacodynamics change in overdose", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty-two. How pharmacodynamics change in overdose. At overdose doses a drug can do things it does not do at therapeutic doses. The slide makes three points. Consider the therapeutic index and the overlap of the therapeutic and toxic response curves. At least part of the toxic effect may be different from the therapeutic action. And beta blockers or calcium channel blockers alter blood flow to the kidney and liver as well as cardiac function.", "s": "treat:5", "r": null}, {"t": "The Katzung figure shows drugs A and B. At the therapeutic dose both cause sedation, at the same dose. At higher doses, drug A rises in a straight line through hypnosis and anesthesia to coma. Drug B rises only to anesthesia and levels off there. So the dose of B can be increased without the unwanted effect, and A is the more dangerous drug in overdose. Nothing at the therapeutic dose tells them apart. Having the same therapeutic dose does not mean equal safety. She said, in overdose, their outcomes are different.", "s": "treat:5", "r": null}, {"t": "Tricyclic antidepressants, the T C As, show a toxic effect that differs from the therapeutic action. Seizures and increased muscle activity generate heat, and sweating would release it. At toxic doses, T C As reduce sweating at the same time as they raise muscle activity and seizure risk. She said, it's a double whammy in an overdose situation. She noted that saving a patient in a T C A overdose is rare.", "s": "treat:5", "r": null}, {"t": "In a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker overdose, the excess cardiac effect lowers blood flow to the liver and kidneys. Those are the organs that eliminate the drug, so clearance falls and the level stays high. She said, reducing blood flow to the kidneys and the liver, you're reducing the body's ability to eliminate the drug. Here a pharmacodynamic change causes a kinetic one, so this overdose is not purely a cardiac problem.", "s": "treat:5", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l15-9": {"title": "Three angles of treatment and preventing absorption", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty-three. Three angles, four toxicokinetic methods, and preventing absorption. A poisoned patient is treated from one of three angles. The kinetics of the poison, inactivating the poison, or the pharmacology of what it is doing. Toxicokinetic treatment aims to decrease the amount of poison in the body, hoping to reduce end-organ damage. It has four methods. Prevent absorption. Inhibit toxication, meaning bioactivation to a more toxic compound. Enhance metabolism, which is detoxification. And enhance elimination. One quiz trap says to enhance metabolism by inducing metabolizing enzymes. That is not correct, because induction takes days.", "s": "treat:7", "r": null}, {"t": "Preventing absorption only works while the poison is still in the gut, and every method carries risk. Emesis with ipecac induces vomiting. She said, aspiration is the biggest risk. Emesis is no longer used, and ipecac is no longer sold in the United States. A quiz statement calling emesis first line for an oral poison is false. Gastric lavage passes a tube to the stomach and pumps fluid in and back out. The tube may go into the lungs, and large tablets will not come up it. An experienced emergency room may try it for a massive, very dangerous ingestion.", "s": "treat:8", "r": null}, {"t": "Whole bowel irrigation means drinking polyethylene glycol solution or magnesium citrate until it passes through, as before a colonoscopy. The gut must be open, with no ileus and no blockage. It is likely to be tried for a massive ingestion. DiPiro lists it for sustained-release or enteric-coated drugs, iron, lithium, potassium and swallowed drug packets.", "s": "treat:8", "r": "dipiro-e9"}, {"t": "Single-dose activated charcoal is a slurry of fine charcoal in water. The poison sticks to it and leaves in the feces. By patient-outcome data it is the best option. It can bind only what is still in the stomach and small intestine, so there is a time window. Her quiz key says less than sixty minutes after ingestion. In lecture she allowed up to two hours. Charcoal does not bind every compound. DiPiro names iron, lead, lithium, alcohols and corrosives among those it does not bind.", "s": "treat:8", "r": "dipiro-e9"}, {"t": "Multiple-dose activated charcoal can work even after absorption, for a drug with enterohepatic recirculation. Such a drug goes from the liver into the bile and back into the small intestine, where another dose of charcoal catches it. She said, the use of multiple dose activated charcoal is limited to things that get stuck on charcoal and undergo that recirculation. The assigned DiPiro chapter reviews these methods to show how their use has changed. It cites the American and European clinical toxicology position statements that no method of gastric decontamination should be used routinely, and she said they should be used only when judged necessary.", "s": "treat:8", "r": "dipiro-e9"}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l16-1": {"title": "Preventing absorption and inhibiting bioactivation", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty five. Preventing absorption, and inhibiting bioactivation. By patient outcome data, activated charcoal is probably the best of the methods that prevent absorption, though it does not bind everything and carries its own risk. A single dose works up to two hours after ingestion, and her Quiz three key says less than sixty minutes. She said, you need to get that charcoal in their stomach and small intestine while the poison is still in that area of the body. Multiple doses are for a poison that charcoal binds and that undergoes enterohepatic recirculation. Each new dose catches drug that the bile puts back into the small intestine.", "s": "treat:8", "r": null}, {"t": "Bioactivation is metabolism that turns what was ingested into something more toxic. If the harm comes from the metabolites, and the first enzyme of the pathway can be blocked, blocking that enzyme keeps the poison in its less harmful parent form. Methanol and ethylene glycol are the classic examples, and there are not many others. Both are first metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase, so one antidote, ethanol or fomepizole, treats both. On Quiz three, fomepizole is classed as inhibition of bioactivation, a toxicokinetic treatment. It blocks the step that makes the toxic metabolite. It does not enhance detoxification or elimination.", "s": "treat:10", "r": null}, {"t": "Methanol goes through alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde, and formaldehyde dehydrogenase turns that into formic acid. Formic acid is the toxic product, not formaldehyde. She said, formic acid will cause damage, especially to the eyes, to the retina of the eyes. Sight is lost. Her key lists anion gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity. Because the damage comes from formic acid and not from methanol itself, preventing the metabolism prevents the blindness. Most people poisoned this way have an alcohol use disorder and cannot get ethanol. Methanol gives the same effect as ethanol, so it is drunk when ethanol is not available.", "s": "treat:11", "r": null}, {"t": "Ethylene glycol goes through alcohol dehydrogenase to glycoaldehyde, then aldehyde dehydrogenase to glycolic acid, and after several more steps it ends as oxalic acid. Oxalic acid precipitates as microcrystals in the small vessels, blocks blood flow, and damages organs. She named the liver and kidneys, and her key says renal toxicity is common. Eye damage belongs to methanol, not to ethylene glycol. Ethylene glycol is antifreeze. It smells and tastes sweet and does not burn going down, so pets and small children drink it. The pink or blue color is a dye.", "s": "treat:11", "r": null}, {"t": "Ethanol and fomepizole both block alcohol dehydrogenase, in different ways. Ethanol outcompetes methanol or ethylene glycol for the enzyme, and emergency rooms once kept a bottle of Everclear and gave a shot or two. Fomepizole binds to the enzyme and inhibits it, with better control of the dosing. Her key says fomepizole is the better choice, because it is a stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor with fewer adverse effects than ethanol.", "s": "treat:11", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l16-2": {"title": "Blood tests and toxidromes", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty six. Recognizing the poisoning. A poisoned patient often cannot or will not say what was taken. An unconscious patient cannot, and people who intended to hurt themselves often do not. So recognition runs in two steps. First, the group of signs and symptoms, called a toxidrome, suggests a drug class. Then, if the hospital laboratory can measure the suspected compound, a blood test confirms it. She said, other than that, sometimes it's just a guess. The toxidrome comes first because symptoms can be seen as soon as the patient arrives.", "s": null, "r": null}, {"t": "Emergency rooms test the blood for the common overdoses that can be detected there. These include acetaminophen, salicylates, iron, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, opiates, and a few others. For methanol or ethylene glycol, the key indicator is a blood draw that looks for these alcohols. The patient may arrive unconscious or sedated.", "s": null, "r": null}, {"t": "The toxidrome table is Goodman and Gilman Table four dash seven. She showed it without walking through its rows, and Quiz three tested the opioid row. Each row follows from what the drug class does. Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up. Her key lists acetylcholine excess, the SLUDGE signs, which are salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastric cramping and emesis, and the row shows small pupils. The row also lists somnolence or coma and fasciculation. Anticholinergic drugs such as diphenhydramine and atropine do the opposite to the pupils, which are large, with delirium, ileus and flushing.", "s": "treat:33", "r": null}, {"t": "Two pairs of rows look alike. Sympathomimetic drugs such as cocaine and amphetamine, and anticholinergic drugs, both raise heart rate, blood pressure, temperature and pupil size. Agitation with tremor and sweating points to sympathomimetic. Delirium with ileus and flushing points to anticholinergic. Opioids such as heroin and oxycodone, and sedative hypnotics such as benzodiazepines and barbiturates, both cause somnolence or coma and slow breathing. Small pupils and a slow heart point to opioid. Low blood pressure without a pupil change points to sedative hypnotic.", "s": "treat:33", "r": null}, {"t": "On Quiz three she asked which symptom is not part of the opioid toxidrome. The answer was increased pupil size. Decreased heart rate, decreased breathing rate and somnolence are part of it, and the pupils are small. This is the picture naloxone reverses. Salicylates such as aspirin bring confusion with raised heart rate, breathing rate and temperature, which her key calls hyperventilation and hyperthermia. A calcium channel blocker overdose such as verapamil lowers heart rate and blood pressure, but her key says the patient is often alert. A beta blocker overdose may alter mental status, which helps tell the two apart.", "s": "treat:33", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l16-3": {"title": "Enhancing metabolism with cofactors", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty seven. Enhancing metabolism with cofactors. One toxicokinetic treatment speeds up the body's own detoxification of the poison. Inducing more metabolizing enzyme would do that, but it is too slow. She said, it takes a few days, so we can't treat our patient fast enough with that. What works quickly is supplying a cofactor or ingredient that an existing detoxifying reaction uses up, so the enzyme already present keeps running faster. On Quiz three, enhancing metabolism by inducing metabolizing enzymes was the statement that is not correct. Her two examples are cyanide and acetaminophen.", "s": "treat:12", "r": null}, {"t": "Cyanide's target is cytochrome c oxidase, an enzyme of the electron transport chain. Cyanide displaces the oxygen bound in the enzyme and stops it from working, so cells stop making A T P, and the patient basically suffocates. Three agents are given in order. First, amyl nitrite oxidizes the iron of hemoglobin, making methemoglobin. Methemoglobin pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase onto itself, so the enzyme is regenerated and cellular respiration resumes. That step is removal of the poison from its active site, not enhanced metabolism. The cyanide is still in the body, now bound to methemoglobin.", "s": "treat:13", "r": null}, {"t": "The second step is the one that enhances metabolism. The body's own enzyme, rhodanese, moves the cyanide off methemoglobin onto sulfur. The agent given is sodium thiosulfate, which donates that sulfur. The rhodanese pathway is capacity limited, so supplying thiosulfate increases how much cyanide rhodanese can convert. The product, labeled thiocyanate, is eliminated by the kidneys. So rhodanese is the enzyme, and sodium thiosulfate is the agent given.", "s": "treat:13", "r": "marraffa"}, {"t": "The third step fixes a problem the first step made. The patient now has a large amount of methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen. Methylene blue is a reducing agent. It converts methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, so the blood can carry oxygen again. Amyl nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, and methylene blue reduces methemoglobin back to hemoglobin.", "s": "treat:13", "r": null}, {"t": "Acetaminophen is metabolized to N A P Q I, a free radical that damages the liver. Glutathione, with the help of the glutathione S transferase enzymes, conjugates N A P Q I so it can be eliminated. N acetylcysteine, called N A C, supplies some of the ingredients the body needs to make more glutathione, so more N A P Q I is conjugated and eliminated. N A C does not neutralize the free radical itself. Her key says it loses effectiveness about eight hours after the overdose, and you still give it. In the United States it is usually given intravenously, because a patient will hardly swallow it.", "s": "treat:14", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l16-4": {"title": "Enhancing elimination: ion trapping and extracorporeal methods", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty eight. Enhancing elimination. Many poisons are filtered at the glomerulus into the forming urine and then reabsorbed back into the blood. She said, so you continue the poisoning by the reabsorption of that compound from the forming urine into the systemic circulation. Enhancing renal elimination means stopping that reabsorption, so the poison leaves in the urine. The common way to do that is ion trapping.", "s": "treat:15", "r": null}, {"t": "In ion trapping, sodium bicarbonate is given intravenously. Bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, raising its p H. A weakly acidic poison, uncharged at normal urine p H, becomes negatively charged in basic urine. Most of the transporters that reabsorb solutes from the urine back into the blood move uncharged molecules, so the charged acid cannot be reabsorbed and is excreted. It is used for weak acids, salicylates and barbiturates such as phenobarbital, and is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital.", "s": "treat:15", "r": null}, {"t": "Ion trapping is not done for weak bases. Trapping a weak base would need acidic urine, and making the urine acidic would mean acidifying the patient's blood, which is dangerous. Cranberry juice acidifies urine, but not to a low enough p H and not fast enough. Bicarbonate also appears in a different role. Her table key gives it in a tricyclic antidepressant overdose for the widened Q R S interval, to counteract inhibition of cardiac sodium channels. For salicylates it is given for ion trapping.", "s": "treat:15", "r": null}, {"t": "When the kidneys cannot clear the poison well enough, the blood can be cleaned outside the body. Hemodialysis, hemofiltration and hemoperfusion artificially clear the toxin from the plasma. They are for very severe poisonings where other treatments are not working. Plasma exchange and exchange transfusion were only named, for patients who are really bad. Dialysis does not remove every poison, and whether it works depends on the chemistry of the poison.", "s": "treat:16", "r": null}, {"t": "These methods work only for toxins with a small volume of distribution. A large volume of distribution means the drug is not staying in the blood vessels. It is distributed and sequestered in places such as fat, muscle and bone. The machine can only clean the blood, so most of the drug stays out of reach and keeps returning to the blood, and removal would take a very long time.", "s": "treat:16", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l16-5": {"title": "Inactivating poisons: chelators, antivenoms and antibodies", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty nine. Inactivating poisons. The first goal in a poisoning is to prevent absorption. Once the poison has been absorbed into the systemic circulation and is not readily eliminated, some poisons can be inactivated. A second molecule binds the poison so it can no longer reach its target, and the poison and inactivator complex is then cleared from the body. This is what she officially calls an antidote. The two groups of inactivators are heavy metal chelators, and antivenoms and antibodies.", "s": "treat:17", "r": null}, {"t": "A good inactivator binds the toxin so that no part of it can still touch the target. It has high affinity for the toxin, because otherwise massive amounts would be needed, and the inactivator could cause side effects of its own. The complex has low toxicity, which she changed to no toxicity, because binding is useless if the bound complex is still poisonous. And the complex must be cleared from the body. On Quiz three, the statement that the complex does not need to be cleared was the false one.", "s": "treat:17", "r": null}, {"t": "Heavy metal chelators bind lead, mercury, cadmium, iron and copper. They are small molecules with a nucleophilic electron donor that binds the metal tightly, forming a metal ligand complex. They are usually multidentate, holding one metal atom at several points. A chelator needs higher affinity for the toxic metal than for the body's own macromolecules. It needs low affinity for endogenous ions, such as magnesium in enzymes and especially calcium, because the body needs those. So a chelator that also binds calcium is a bad chelator. It must also be water soluble, non toxic and readily cleared.", "s": "treat:18", "r": null}, {"t": "Each chelating agent pairs with the metals it binds. Two bind iron: deferoxamine, which her poisoning table key says chelates the circulating ferric iron in an iron overdose, and deferasirox, the oral one, recently approved by the Food and Drug Administration. Two bind copper: penicillamine and trientine, usually for Wilson's disease, a genetic disease with very high copper levels that damage organs. Three bind lead. Edetate disodium binds lead. Dimercaprol, also called British anti Lewisite, binds lead, mercury, arsenic and gold. Succimer binds lead, mercury, arsenic and cadmium.", "s": "treat:20", "r": null}, {"t": "Antivenoms and antibodies need high affinity and high specificity for what they bind. A polyclonal antivenom is made by inoculating an animal with small amounts of snake venom, not enough to kill it. Its immune system makes antibodies, its blood is drawn, and those antibodies are filtered out. The product is a collection of antibodies, not one single antibody. The patient's body then reacts against the many foreign animal antibodies while also fighting the venom. The result is a type three hypersensitivity reaction, called serum sickness, with fever, nausea and malaise.", "s": "treat:21", "r": null}, {"t": "Digoxin immune Fab is the one antibody that treats a drug overdose. Her table key says these digoxin specific antibody fragments bind digoxin and are eliminated by the kidneys. After Fab is given, measuring serum digoxin does not help. The total digoxin concentration, free plus bound to Fab, rises, so it cannot be used to calculate further Fab doses.", "s": "treat:21", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l16-6": {"title": "Pharmacological treatment in four categories", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty. Pharmacological treatment. The pharmacodynamic way to treat a poisoning is to counteract what the poison does. You give a drug with the opposite pharmacological action, or something that goes around a metabolic pathway the poison has blocked. The four categories are a receptor antagonist, a receptor agonist, removal of the poison from the active site, and a bypass of an inhibited pathway. Each category is defined by what the poison does at its target and what the treatment does about it.", "s": "treat:22", "r": null}, {"t": "When the poison acts as an agonist, the treatment is a receptor antagonist. Naloxone is an antagonist at the opioid receptor and treats opioid overdose. Flumazenil is an antagonist at the GABA A benzodiazepine receptor and treats benzodiazepine overdose. She slipped while lecturing, then settled it. She said, the benzos are the overdose, flumazenil is your antidote, and your treatment is the antagonist. Her key adds that flumazenil can cause withdrawal in a benzodiazepine dependent patient, and naloxone can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users.", "s": "treat:23", "r": null}, {"t": "Atropine is the third antagonist example. Organophosphate pesticides inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up in the synaptic cleft and causes cholinergic excess. Atropine antagonizes acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, blocking the receptors the excess acetylcholine is over stimulating. Organophosphates also have a second treatment in a different category, pralidoxime, and soldiers' field kits carry both mixed in one.", "s": "treat:24", "r": null}, {"t": "When the poison acts as an antagonist, the treatment acts as an agonist. Belladonna alkaloids, as in jimsonweed, cause anticholinergic effects such as dilated pupils. Physostigmine blocks acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine increases, and its net effect is that of a receptor agonist, even though it is an enzyme inhibitor. Atropine is the antagonist, for cholinergic excess. Physostigmine is the agonist category treatment, for anticholinergic poisoning.", "s": "treat:25", "r": null}, {"t": "The third category removes the poison from the active site of an enzyme or protein. For cyanide, methemoglobin made by amyl nitrite pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase onto itself. For organophosphates, pralidoxime pulls the organophosphate off acetylcholinesterase onto itself, restoring the enzyme, and the complex is eliminated. Pralidoxime sometimes fails because of aging. Some organophosphates, after binding acetylcholinesterase, lose part of their phosphate group. The aged organophosphate is bound permanently, and pralidoxime can no longer pull it off. Whether a given organophosphate ages depends on its chemical structure.", "s": "treat:26", "r": null}, {"t": "The fourth category bypasses an inhibited pathway. Vitamin K dependent carboxylase activates some coagulation factors by carboxylating them, using active vitamin K as a cofactor. Used vitamin K becomes an inactive epoxide, and epoxide reductase regenerates the active form. Warfarin inhibits epoxide reductase, not the carboxylase, so there is less active vitamin K and fewer active coagulation factors. Giving vitamin K supplies the active form directly and goes around the blocked enzyme, so it is not an antagonist of warfarin. Over anticoagulation shows as bruising, gastrointestinal bleeds or hemorrhagic stroke.", "s": "treat:28", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l16-7": {"title": "Beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose guidelines", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty one. Out of hospital management of beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose. Someone is found at home after swallowing a handful of one of these drugs, and a family member calls the poison center or nine one one. Two guidelines from the American Association of Poison Control Centers, published in two thousand five, tell poison center staff what to say on that call. Wax and colleagues wrote the beta blocker guideline, and Olson and colleagues wrote the calcium channel blocker guideline. Out of hospital means the period before the patient reaches a healthcare facility.", "s": "treat:30", "r": "aapcc-bb"}, {"t": "There are no clinical trials behind these guidelines, because no one can ethically be consented to swallow an overdose so that its treatment can be studied. The data come from real overdoses that clinicians write up and publish. The authors pulled every published case report and case series, graded the strength of each piece of evidence, and then graded each recommendation. All seven calcium channel blocker recommendations are Grade D. No new treatments have appeared since, so there has been no reason to rewrite them.", "s": "treat:31", "r": "aapcc-ccb"}, {"t": "The formulation matters because of absorption. Her key says immediate release products are absorbed more rapidly because they do not tend to form bezoars, so absorption is quick and complete. Extended release tablets stick together in an overdose and clump into a mass, called a bezoar, in the stomach or small intestine. The clump can break apart at an unpredictable time, hours after the ingestion, and release a large amount of drug at once. So a patient with no symptoms after an extended release overdose is not safe as early as one after an immediate release overdose. Slow release is true of these tablets, but she called the clumping the biggest issue.", "s": null, "r": "aapcc-bb", "tb": 0}, {"t": "For a patient with no symptoms, the decision depends on the formulation and on the hours since ingestion. The window is the number of hours during which a patient without symptoms still goes to the hospital. Immediate release products have a six hour window for both classes. Sotalol is excluded from that rule, because it also blocks the potassium channel, and the beta blocker guideline gives it twelve hours. A sustained release beta blocker has an eight hour window.", "s": null, "r": "aapcc-bb", "tb": 0}, {"t": "A modified release calcium channel blocker has an eighteen hour window, except verapamil. Modified release verapamil has twenty four hours. She said, it actually has multiple mechanisms. Because it is not as clean a calcium channel blocker as the others, toxicity is a concern for longer, and a patient with no symptoms ten hours after modified release verapamil is not out of danger. Immediate release verapamil follows the six hour rule. Eight belongs to sustained release beta blockers, and eighteen to modified release calcium channel blockers.", "s": null, "r": "aapcc-ccb", "tb": 0}, {"t": "That difference between the two classes comes from the published case data, and she said it speaks to the mechanism of action. The guidelines describe the two mechanisms. Beta blockers competitively block beta adrenergic receptors, which decreases cyclic A M P and blunts the effects of catecholamines. Calcium channel blockers block calcium entry through voltage sensitive calcium channels, which relaxes arterial smooth muscle and depresses the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes and cardiac contractility.", "s": null, "r": "aapcc-ccb", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Inside the window, a patient with no symptoms goes to the hospital, because the drug may not all be absorbed yet, and an unknown amount is a reason to go in. The guidelines also set a dose cutoff. Referral is considered when the amount is more than the usual maximum single therapeutic dose or equals the lowest reported toxic dose, whichever is lower. After the window, call the poison center. The patient can probably be managed at home with someone watching them. A patient with symptoms goes to the hospital at any time. The beta blocker guideline gives follow up calls for up to twelve to twenty four hours, while the calcium channel blocker guideline leaves follow up to the poison center's judgment. Suspected self harm goes to the emergency department, then to mental health care, whatever the time or the dose.", "s": null, "r": null, "tb": 1}, {"t": "At home, do not induce vomiting. A vomiting patient can breathe the vomit into the lungs, which she called the biggest risk of emesis. Activated charcoal is considered if there are no contraindications, but it is given at the hospital and never delays transport. Single dose charcoal works only soon after ingestion, less than sixty minutes on her Quiz three key and up to two hours in her lecture, so time spent arranging it is time the patient is not on the way. Go by ambulance, not the family car. If the patient crashes in a private car, nobody has intravenous lines, pressors or defibrillator paddles, and the ambulance carries all of these.", "s": null, "r": null, "tb": 1}, {"t": "The calcium channel blocker guideline adds what can be given during transport, if available: intravenous fluids for hypotension, and intravenous calcium, glucagon and epinephrine for severe hypotension.", "s": null, "r": "aapcc-ccb", "tb": 1}], "mins": 5}, "guide3-l16-8": {"title": "The poisoning treatment table", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty two. The poisoning treatment table. The lecture taught the general ways to treat a poisoning, and the table applies them to specific overdoses. For each one it lists the signs of acute poisoning and how it is diagnosed, the antidote, how the antidote works, and a special consideration. Her reference column cites the Marraffa article, Antidotes for toxicological emergencies, for most rows, Katzung for tricyclics and salicylates, and DiPiro for iron and organophosphates. She said the Marraffa article is the quickest to read, and that these papers have not been replaced by anything better.", "s": null, "r": "marraffa", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Each antidote fits a lecture category. Fomepizole and ethanol inhibit bioactivation of the toxic alcohols, and fomepizole is the stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor with fewer adverse effects. N acetylcysteine enhances metabolism by replenishing glutathione. It loses effectiveness about eight hours after an acetaminophen overdose, and you still give it. Sodium bicarbonate for salicylates enhances elimination. Deferoxamine for iron and the digoxin antibody fragments inactivate the poison. Flumazenil, naloxone and atropine are antagonists, and pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase.", "s": null, "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers share one antidote list. Both cause hypotension and bradycardia, so the treatments aim at heart rate, calcium entry, contractility and blood pressure. Atropine increases heart rate. Beta blockers decrease cyclic A M P production, and glucagon increases cardiac cyclic A M P to raise the heart rate. Calcium given as a treatment drives calcium into cardiac cells, and vasopressors raise blood pressure. High dose insulin, hyperinsulinemia euglycemia therapy or H I E T, has an inotropic effect whose mechanism is not understood, and it was Quiz three's answer for an extended release calcium channel blocker overdose. Intravenous twenty percent fat emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. A beta blocker overdose may cause altered mental status, while a calcium channel blocker patient is often alert.", "s": null, "r": "aapcc-bb", "tb": 0}, {"t": "Sodium bicarbonate has two different jobs in the table. For salicylates it is renal ion trapping. For tricyclic antidepressants it treats the prolonged Q R S interval by counteracting inhibition of cardiac sodium channels. Salicylates cause hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis, with hyperthermia, confusion and seizures.", "s": null, "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "Digoxin overdose brings bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, nausea and vomiting. After the antibody fragments are given, serum digoxin is unhelpful, because total digoxin, free plus bound, rises. Iron has a timeline. In the first one to six hours come vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea. From six to twenty four hours come lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting and diarrhea, and shock. At two to five days, liver dysfunction and failure are possible. Deferoxamine chelates the circulating ferric iron.", "s": null, "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "Four more rows. Acetaminophen brings nausea and vomiting and clinical signs of liver failure, and blood levels can be measured. Benzodiazepines bring central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs, and blood levels can be measured. Tricyclic antidepressants act on the heart, with tachycardia, a prolonged Q R S interval and reduced contractility, and also bring dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures and sometimes vasodilation; treatment is mostly supportive, with intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine to maintain blood pressure. Sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdoses cause hypoglycemia, and octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas.", "s": null, "r": null, "tb": 0}], "mins": 4}, "guide3-l17-1": {"title": "Why pharmacogenomics exists", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty three. Why pharmacogenomics. People given the same drug can respond very differently. The same drug can be lethal in one person and do nothing in another. These differences were first linked to age, ethnicity, gender and underlying disease. As knowledge of genetics grew, some of the variation could be explained by genetic variation. Pharmacogenomics exists to explain and predict that part. The early evidence came before anyone could genotype. Scientists measured a phenotype, such as an enzyme's activity or a drug's half-life, and showed that it was inherited.", "s": "pgx:4", "r": null}, {"t": "The first real data point was primaquine, an antimalarial given widely to soldiers as prophylaxis during the Korean War in the nineteen fifties. Some soldiers developed hemolytic anemia, and those of Middle Eastern or African descent seemed most at risk. The soldiers who reacted badly had low activity of the enzyme glucose six phosphate dehydrogenase in their red blood cells. Genotyping was not possible then, but enzyme activity could be measured. She said, we couldn't genotype at that time, but we could measure that enzyme activity. That low activity phenotype reflected a genetic variation, so this was the first link between a bad drug response and genetics.", "s": "pgx:5", "r": null}, {"t": "Now the timeline. In nineteen fifty three, Watson and Crick and others described the structure of D N A. In nineteen fifty six, a genetic link was found for the hemolytic anemia caused by primaquine. In nineteen fifty seven, Motulsky said that inheritance might explain many individual differences in drug efficacy and adverse drug reactions. In nineteen fifty nine, Vogel coined the term pharmacogenetics. Motulsky made the statement; Vogel coined the word. The Human Genome Project ran from nineteen ninety to two thousand three and finished early and under budget. Whole genome sequencing came after the project, made possible by the faster methods the project developed.", "s": "pgx:6", "r": null}, {"t": "Twin studies showed the same thing with a drug. The half-life of antipyrine was compared in identical twin pairs and in fraternal twin pairs. Antipyrine was chosen because it is cleared entirely by metabolism, by one known enzyme, so its half-life is a clean measure of that enzyme's activity. Identical twins had almost identical half-lives. Fraternal pairs varied, with only one pair close. Identical twins share their genotype, and fraternal twins do not. So if the same genotype gives the same clearance and different genotypes give different clearances, the variation in clearance is inherited.", "s": "pgx:7", "r": null}, {"t": "The trap in both stories is the same. Neither the soldiers nor the twins were genotyped. Both studies measured a phenotype. She said, we were looking at their phenotype, which was the half-life of the clearance of the drug. And for the twins, the fraternal pairs are not a match. Only the identical twins matched.", "s": "pgx:7", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l17-2": {"title": "Pharmacogenetics versus pharmacogenomics, and why it matters now", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty four. Pharmacogenetics versus pharmacogenomics. The two terms differ in scope. Pharmacogenetics is the study of genetic causes of individual variations in drug response: one patient and their response to a drug. Pharmacogenomics more broadly involves genome-wide analysis of the genetic determinants of drug efficacy and toxicity. It covers populations as a whole, or comparisons between populations, and it includes genomics technologies such as gene therapy. She said, pharmacogenomics is a more global term. The two are used interchangeably, and the preferred term is pharmacogenomics.", "s": "pgx:8", "r": null}, {"t": "The slide definitions come from the American Association of Pharmaceutical Scientists Pharmacogenomics Focus Group. Why do organizations define terms at all? To get everyone on the same page. If agencies define genotypes or sample populations differently, the data get messy, and one country's agency cannot compare its data with another's. Shared definitions give better scientific communication.", "s": "pgx:8", "r": null}, {"t": "Two papers show the impact. MacKinnon and colleagues, two thousand twenty three, found that more than half the patients evaluated were prescribed medications where pharmacogenomics is important in their use. The trap is to say pharmacogenomics was used in half the patients. It was not evaluated. The study reviewed drugs, adverse effects and efficacy in medical records. Wondrasek and colleagues, two thousand twenty four, found that pharmacists feel pharmacogenomics is important but do not feel confident in their knowledge of it or their ability to use it. Pharmacy students are the best educated in it. Prescribers should know it too, but are not yet as well versed, because they do not spend as much time with drugs.", "s": "pgx:9", "r": null}, {"t": "When do you genotype? Often a side effect or a lack of efficacy prompts genotyping afterward, and that is acceptable when the side effect is not lethal. But if a drug can quickly cause a lethal side effect, you genotype before giving it. For some medications this is standard protocol, and not doing it could make you liable, because of how severe the toxicity can be. She said, it's better to genotype the patient, figure out what version of a gene they have before you give them that drug. Oncology uses pharmacogenomics all the time, and the central nervous system, cardiology and infectious disease use it increasingly.", "s": null, "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l17-3": {"title": "The four target areas and the two outcomes", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty five. The four target areas. Genetic variation affects drug response through four kinds of proteins. She said, metabolizing enzymes, transporters, drug targets, and then immunological targets. She added immune system targets the morning of the lecture, and the slide title was not updated from three. The answer is four. Metabolizing enzymes came first and are the best characterized. A patient with a low activity enzyme cannot clear the drug, so the blood concentration rises too high, and some patients died. Those reactions were the most apparent and the most dangerous, so they were explained first. The slide adds that these variants may have serious implications for narrow therapeutic index drugs.", "s": "pgx:10", "r": null}, {"t": "Transporters move drugs across cells in the gut, liver, brain and elsewhere. Her example is P glycoprotein, encoded by the gene A B C B one, an efflux transporter in the gut. It pumps a substrate drug back into the gut lumen, so less of an oral dose reaches the blood. Digoxin is a P glycoprotein substrate with a very narrow therapeutic index, and its dose assumes normal transporter activity. In a patient with a low activity variant, less digoxin is pumped back, more is absorbed, and bioavailability goes up. The trap says digoxin levels fall. They rise.", "s": "pgx:10", "r": null}, {"t": "Drug targets are where a drug acts: a receptor, or an enzyme the drug inhibits. If a receptor variant responds poorly, a drug acting on it works poorly. Her receptor example is a guidance document on beta adrenergic receptor variants. In oncology, if a patient's cancer does not express the protein a cancer drug binds, the drug has nothing to bind and will not work. This area is moving more slowly and has its biggest role now in oncology. The fourth area is immune system targets, the H L A antigens. Certain H L A variants make patients respond badly to certain drugs, and that list grows every year.", "s": "pgx:10", "r": null}, {"t": "The Pharm G K B figure sorts all of this into two arcs. The top arc is pharmacokinetics: enzymes and transporters change absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion. Its usual outcome is toxicity, for example a less active enzyme leaving drug levels too high. The bottom arc is pharmacodynamics: the drug target changes the response. Its usual outcome is efficacy, for example a cancer without the drug's target protein. That scenario belongs in the efficacy bucket, not toxicity. She said, we always try to either prevent toxicity or ensure efficacy by using pharmacogenomics.", "s": "pgx:11", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l17-4": {"title": "Wild type, variant, polymorphism, rare variant and S N P", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty six. Wild type, variant, polymorphism, rare variant and S N P. These terms name versions of a gene by how common they are. Wild type is the version of a gene thought to be the most common. Sometimes the literature's wild type is only the version scientists understood first, but she accepts the most common as the definition. A variant is a version of the gene not thought to be common. Say variant, not mutant. She uses neutral words for genetic changes, and mutant is no longer commonly used.", "s": "pgx:13", "r": "katzung-5"}, {"t": "Polymorphism and rare variant sort variants by frequency, with the cutoff at one percent. A genetic variant present in at least one percent of the population is a polymorphism. One present in less than one percent is a rare variant. She said, by definition, a polymorphism occurs at least one percent of the time in a population. A single nucleotide polymorphism, an S N P, pronounced snip, is one nucleotide that differs from the wild type sequence. It is the most common type of polymorphism. But S N P names a kind of change, not a frequency.", "s": "pgx:14", "r": null}, {"t": "That is why a whole gene deletion can be a polymorphism. She said, polymorphism definition speaks to the frequency of that change happening. More than one nucleotide changed, an entire gene inserted or deleted, extra copies of a gene: any of these is a polymorphism if it occurs in at least one percent of the population, and a rare variant if less. So the statement that polymorphisms are only single nucleotide changes is false. And a variant that occurs very frequently describes a polymorphism, not a haplotype.", "s": "pgx:14", "r": null}, {"t": "Why did the term rare variant appear? For a long time the work focused on polymorphisms, and known polymorphisms explained many differences in drug response. Then some patients reacted badly to a drug without carrying the known polymorphism. Their genotype showed an uncommon variation found in less than one percent of people. The variant is rare, but the bad reaction is just as real for that patient, so these variants now get attention.", "s": "pgx:14", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l17-5": {"title": "Allele, genotype, phenotype and functional significance", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty seven. Allele, genotype, phenotype and functional significance. Humans are diploid: two copies of every chromosome, so two copies of each gene, one from the biological mother and one from the father. Each copy is an allele, the variant or wild type form of a gene at a particular location on a chromosome.", "s": "pgx:15", "r": null}, {"t": "When the two alleles at a locus are the same, the person is homozygous. In the figure, capital P capital P and lowercase a lowercase a are homozygous. When they differ, the person is heterozygous, as with capital B and lowercase b at the third locus. Dominant and recessive appear in the figure, but they are not used in pharmacogenomics. Gene dose replaces them later in the course.", "s": "pgx:16", "r": null}, {"t": "The two alleles a person has for a gene are the genotype. You write the gene, then both alleles: C Y P two C nineteen star two, star two, which is homozygous for the star two allele. The phenotype is any observable characteristic, and it results from the expression of genes. C Y P two C nineteen star two has no activity, so a person with two star two alleles has no C Y P two C nineteen activity and cannot metabolize drugs through that enzyme. The trap swaps them: the allele pair is the genotype, and no enzyme activity is the phenotype.", "s": "pgx:17", "r": null}, {"t": "Functional significance is what a variant does to how the gene's product works, whether a protein, an enzyme or a transporter. It is not how often the variant occurs; that is frequency. She said, phenotypes are driven by the genotype. But genotype drives phenotype only through the protein. If we know what a variant does to the protein's function, we can predict the drug response phenotype and decide whether a patient should get a drug, or whether the dose should be reduced. If we do not know it, we cannot explain the phenotype. For C Y P two C nineteen star two, the relationship is really tight.", "s": "pgx:17", "r": null}, {"t": "Three things blur the link between genotype and phenotype. First, many polymorphisms in a single gene: with variants at several places, it is hard to tell which one causes the phenotype. Second, most traits are multigenic, not monogenic. A few diseases, such as sickle cell and Huntington's chorea, come from one variant, but most, such as cardiovascular disease and diabetes, come from several. Drug response can also be multigenic, with each gene's variant carrying its own weight. Third, environment: diet, sleep, stress and epigenetic factors such as D N A methylation change how genes are expressed, and expression changes the effect of a variant.", "s": "pgx:18", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide3-l17-6": {"title": "Haplotype, frequencies and Hardy Weinberg equilibrium", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty eight. Haplotype, frequencies and Hardy Weinberg equilibrium. A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci that coexist on the same chromosome. More precisely, it is more than one genetic variant grouped together, co-existing more often than chance would predict. That more often than chance part has a name: linkage disequilibrium. Co-existing on one chromosome is not enough on its own. And one variant at one locus that occurs more often than chance is not a haplotype, because a haplotype needs multiple loci. A single variant in at least one percent of people is a polymorphism; a group inherited together is a haplotype.", "s": "pgx:19", "r": "katzung-5"}, {"t": "In the illustration, three loci each carry a single nucleotide change: A or G at the first, G or T at the second, A or T at the third. Among people with variants at these loci, the changes appear together in three patterns: A G T, G T A and A G A. Each pattern is a haplotype. Something about the structure and chemistry of that stretch of D N A makes those changes occur in those groupings, so they appear together more often than chance. Warfarin will be the haplotype example later in the course.", "s": "pgx:20", "r": null}, {"t": "Allele frequency is how often one version of the gene, wild type or variant, occurs in a population, as a proportion or percentage. Genotype frequency is how often a combination of two alleles occurs. Hardy Weinberg equilibrium says allele and genotype frequencies stay constant from generation to generation unless specific influences are introduced. Her example: if this class's drug response differed from every past graduate's, and the cause, say stress, had changed the class's genotype, the class would be out of equilibrium, and its data could not be applied to the general population. So pharmacogenomic papers state that their population was in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, to show their results are generalizable.", "s": "pgx:21", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide3-l17-7": {"title": "Every pharmacogenomics term through frequencies", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirty nine. Every pharmacogenomics term through frequencies. Her objective, define pharmacogenomics terminology, covers every term on every slide, not only the terms the objectives name.", "s": "pgx:2", "r": null}, {"t": "That covers the history and the current uses and limitations of pharmacogenomics. Then the terms: wild type, variant, polymorphism, rare variant, S N P, allele, homozygous, heterozygous, genotype, phenotype, functional significance, haplotype, linkage disequilibrium, allele and genotype frequency, and Hardy Weinberg equilibrium.", "s": "pgx:2", "r": null}], "mins": 1}, "guide4-l18-1": {"title": "Four ways to name a genetic variant", "segs": [{"t": "Section one. Naming genetic variants. The same genetic variant can be written four ways, depending on which system the scientists who first described it chose. Papers, guidance documents and genotype reports use all four, so a pharmacist has to read each one and know what it tells you: where the change is, what the wild type is, and what the variant is. One more convention applies to every gene name. The gene symbol is written in italics and the protein is not. So T P M T in italics is the gene, and T P M T in plain type is the enzyme, thiopurine methyltransferase.", "s": "pgx:22", "r": null}, {"t": "The first system is allele numeric or alphabetic nomenclature. V K O R C one, eleven seventy three C, and V K O R C one, eleven seventy three T, name two versions of the same spot. You get the gene symbol, the nucleotide position counted from the start codon, and the nucleotide found there. You do not get which one is the wild type. Only a writer who follows the convention, wild type in a capital letter and the variant in lowercase, tells you that.", "s": "pgx:23", "r": null}, {"t": "The S N P system, for single nucleotide polymorphism, fixes that. V K O R C one, eleven seventy three, C to T, gives the gene, the position, the wild type nucleotide first, an arrow, and then the variant. A second example is A B C B one, thirty four thirty five, C to T. A B C B one is the gene for P-glycoprotein. The trap is reading the order backward. In C to T, C is the wild type, because the wild type is always written first.", "s": "pgx:24", "r": null}, {"t": "The star system writes the gene, then a star and a number for the allele. She said, We always call star one for whatever gene we're talking about, that's the wild type version. The name does not say where the change is or what it is, so you need a table that defines each star allele. Star numbers follow the order of discovery, not position. C Y P two C nineteen star two, six eighty one G to A, was characterized first. Star three, six thirty six G to A, was found later, yet lies closer to the start codon. And each lab numbers its own gene's variants, so C Y P two C nine star two is not at six eighty one.", "s": "pgx:25", "r": null}, {"t": "The reference S N P system, or r s, gives each S N P a number from the S N P database, d b S N P, that no other S N P has. The Human Genome Variation Society recommends it as the standard. C Y P two C nineteen star three, which is C Y P two C nineteen six thirty six G to A, is r s four nine eight, six eight nine three. The number names one S N P and nothing more. The gene, the position and the wild type all require searching a database that is hard to query. So of the four systems, r s is the one that does not easily let you know which allele is the wild type.", "s": "pgx:27", "r": null}], "mins": 4}, "guide4-l18-2": {"title": "Functional significance and the gene dose effect", "segs": [{"t": "Section two. Functional significance and the gene dose effect. An allele name is only useful once its functional significance is known, meaning what the variant does to the activity of the protein the gene codes for, whether an enzyme, a transporter or a receptor. Functional significance is about activity, not about how common the variant is. For these variants, labs have expressed the variant enzymes and measured their activity against the wild type, so genotype and phenotype are tightly linked.", "s": "pgx:26", "r": null}, {"t": "The same star number can mean different things in different genes. Star one is normal, wild type activity in both C Y P two C nineteen and C Y P two C nine. In C Y P two C nineteen, star two and star three have no activity. Both make a really short, truncated protein that does not function. In C Y P two C nine, star two and star three have decreased activity. So star two does not mean no activity in every gene.", "s": "pgx:26", "r": null}, {"t": "Each person carries two alleles of each gene, one from each parent, and the pair is the genotype, written as C Y P two C nineteen star one, star two. The activity of the two copies adds up. Star one, star one is normal, because both copies make active enzyme. Star one with star two, or star one with star three, is reduced, about halfway, because one copy makes active enzyme and the other makes none. She said, If you were to add it up, they look halfway in the range of two C nineteen activity. She called this the gene dose effect. There is no dominance in these drug-response variants.", "s": "pgx:28", "r": null}, {"t": "Any pairing of the two inactive alleles, star two, star two, star two, star three, or star three, star three, has no activity. Being heterozygous does not by itself mean partial activity; star two, star three has none, because neither copy works. Her own true or false example is that someone homozygous for the star three variant of C Y P two C nineteen has C Y P two C nineteen activity. That is false. Induction by diet or other drugs cannot rescue star two either. No activity comes from star two, because that protein is too short to function.", "s": "pgx:28", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l18-3": {"title": "Haplotypes: V K O R C one and T P M T star three A", "segs": [{"t": "Section three. Haplotype nomenclature. A haplotype is a set of variants at several loci on one allele that occur together in the population more often than random chance. When the same combination of changes keeps turning up in different people, it is common enough to be worth one name, and one name is easier to say and read than the list of changes. Whoever discovers a haplotype names it, so the naming differs from gene to gene.", "s": "pgx:29", "r": null}, {"t": "V K O R C one has at least ten S N Ps that may have a functional effect, and they group together in different ways. She said, There's one called haplotype A, and there's another called haplotype B. Haplotype B is the most common. These haplotypes matter for their functional significance, and V K O R C one comes back later as the gene she pairs with warfarin dosing.", "s": "pgx:29", "r": null}, {"t": "T P M T, thiopurine methyltransferase, uses a star name with a letter instead. The allele was first called T P M T star three. She said, We did some more work and realized that three is actually two snips in the gene. So it was renamed star three A. The two changes on one allele are six fifteen, G to A, and eight seventy four, A to G. Together they give significantly reduced T P M T activity, which matters for azathioprine and six mercaptopurine.", "s": "pgx:35", "r": null}, {"t": "A haplotype is several variants together on one allele, while a genotype is the two alleles a person carries. Not every gene's haplotypes are lettered. Naming is up to the discoverers, and some get star names, like T P M T star three A. V K O R C one is the gene she pairs with warfarin, and T P M T goes with azathioprine and six mercaptopurine.", "s": null, "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l18-4": {"title": "Synonymous, non-synonymous and premature stop S N Ps", "segs": [{"t": "Section four. S N P types in coding regions. A single nucleotide polymorphism is one base pair changed in a gene. Several million are known, and many change nothing about the gene's product. Coding regions are the parts of a gene translated to R N A and then to protein. A coding S N P is classed by what the base change does to the codon. It can give the same amino acid, which is synonymous, a different amino acid, which is non-synonymous, or a stop codon early in the sequence, a premature stop.", "s": "pgx:32", "r": null}, {"t": "The synonymous example is A B C B one, thirty four thirty five, C to T, in the gene for P-glycoprotein. The codon changes from A T C to A T T, but several codons code for the same amino acid, and both give isoleucine. She said, Synonymous means the same. Synonymous does not mean harmless, though. This S N P keeps turning up in drug-response studies with different P-glycoprotein substrates, so it is studied. She thinks expression of the protein is probably affected, but there is no clear consensus on an effect on function or expression. The affected drugs are efavirenz and cyclosporine.", "s": "pgx:34", "r": null}, {"t": "The non-synonymous example is T P M T star three A. At six fifteen, G to A turns G C A, alanine, into A C A, threonine. At eight seventy four, A to G turns T A T, tyrosine, into T G T, cysteine. Each change puts a different amino acid into the enzyme, so its activity is significantly reduced. Azathioprine and six mercaptopurine depend on T P M T. She said, if you don't know the genotype of your patient, you could kill your patient if you didn't genotype them for this snip.", "s": "pgx:35", "r": null}, {"t": "The premature stop codon example is C Y P two C nineteen star three. A G to A change turns T G G, tryptophan, into T A G, a stop codon. T A G tells the cell's machinery to stop translating the R N A, and here it comes far earlier than the normal stop codon. The protein is truncated, so the activity is none, not low. She first called this S N P synonymous and corrected herself, because it changes the sequence by creating a stop codon. The affected drugs are the proton pump inhibitors omeprazole and lansoprazole.", "s": "pgx:36", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l18-5": {"title": "InDels, tandem repeats, gene deletion and copy number", "segs": [{"t": "Section five. Insertions, deletions, repeats and copy number. Not every variant is a single base change. In a small insertion or deletion, called an indel, one, two, three or a few more nucleotides are inserted or deleted in a coding region, as when A T G C becomes A T G G C. Codons are read three bases at a time, so adding or removing a number of bases that is not a multiple of three shifts every later codon. She said, you end up causing a frame shift. The amino acids from that point on are wrong, a premature stop codon may appear, and activity can change. Removing or adding exactly three nucleotides removes or adds one amino acid.", "s": "pgx:37", "r": null}, {"t": "Tandem repeats, or variable number tandem repeats, are a short sequence repeated again and again in a row, and the number of repeats differs between people. Microsatellites have a repeat unit under five base pairs and are also called short tandem repeats or simple sequence repeats. Minisatellites have units over five base pairs. Between genes, repeats often change no function, because there is no protein sequence to change. Inside a coding sequence, a repeat adds bases the way an indel does and can change the protein. Repeats are used in forensic and paternity testing.", "s": "pgx:38", "r": null}, {"t": "C Y P two D six star five is a gene deletion. The whole C Y P two D six gene, thousands of base pairs, is missing from one allele. Star one with star five still has some activity, because the other chromosome has a normal copy that makes enzyme. Star five with star five has no C Y P two D six activity, so drugs cleared by it build up. That is the poor metabolizer phenotype.", "s": "pgx:39", "r": null}, {"t": "C Y P two D six star two times N is the opposite. It is a copy number variant, extra copies of the whole gene, with N listed as two, three, four, five or thirteen. Two copies is written star two times two. The person makes much more enzyme, so drugs cleared by C Y P two D six are metabolized very quickly. She said, we just started calling those people ultra rapid metabolizers. Both variants affect the same drugs, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tamoxifen, codeine and beta blockers. Star five is the deletion and the poor metabolizer. Star two times N is the extra copies and the ultrarapid metabolizer.", "s": "pgx:40", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l18-6": {"title": "Genotype-guided dosing for patients A to D", "segs": [{"t": "Section six. Genotype-guided dosing. When a drug is cleared by a metabolizing enzyme, the patient's genotype for that enzyme predicts how fast the drug is removed, and that rate is a phenotype. Give everyone the same dose and the exposures differ widely. Adjust each dose to the genotype and everyone gets the same exposure. She said, the goal of pharmacogenomics is to ensure efficacy and prevent toxicity.", "s": "pgxex:6", "r": null}, {"t": "The figure follows four patients. Gray bars are the same dose for everyone, one hundred percent of average. White bars are genotype-based doses. Patient A has both alleles non-functional; she described them as having no two D six. A standard dose cannot be cleared, so the concentration goes too high. A lower than average dose prevents toxicity. Patient D has extra copies of the gene. A standard dose is metabolized away, so the A U C, the area under the curve, is very low and never therapeutic. A much higher dose ensures efficacy.", "s": "pgxex:6", "r": null}, {"t": "Patient B has one non-functional allele and gets a dose slightly below average. Patient C has two functional alleles and gets a dose above average. All four end with the same therapeutic A U C, because each dose has been matched to that genotype's metabolizing capability. Extra copies do not mean toxicity; they clear the drug faster, so the risk is no effect. Patient A is toxicity prevented with a low dose, and patient D is efficacy ensured with a high dose.", "s": "pgxex:6", "r": null}, {"t": "An earlier figure shows the same principle with data for warfarin. S-warfarin unbound clearance falls step by step across C Y P two C nine genotypes, from star one, star one at the top, through the genotypes carrying star two or star three, down to star three, star three, the lowest.", "s": "pgxex:5", "r": null}, {"t": "In the Pharm G K B information flow, genes may act on pharmacokinetics, meaning absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, or on pharmacodynamics, meaning the target, the mechanism and the response. Whichever side a gene acts on, the clinical outcome is framed the same way, as efficacy or toxicity.", "s": "pgxex:7", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l18-7": {"title": "Gene structure and frequencies across populations", "segs": [{"t": "Section seven. Gene structure and frequencies across populations. Two figures from Goodman and Gilman's close the day. The first reviews the parts of a gene where variants can occur. The promoter is where the machinery for expressing the gene starts. Exons are the D N A that ends up as amino acid sequence. Introns were once thought meaningless, but variations are now known to occur there. A stop codon sits at the end. Variants are sorted into those changing protein abundance, those changing the protein product, and markers for nearby or distant functional variation.", "s": "pgxex:8", "r": null}, {"t": "The second figure shows that how often each allele or phenotype occurs differs widely between populations. The point is that frequencies vary. For C Y P three A five, star one is the wild type, and star three, star six and star seven are non-functional. In sub-Saharan African populations, star one is common and all three variants are present. In European populations there are relatively few wild type alleles, and most are star three.", "s": "pgxex:9", "r": null}, {"t": "For V K O R C one minus sixteen thirty nine, G to A, the G allele means a higher dose is required and the A allele a lower dose, and the two are compared across African, European and North American, and East Asian populations. For T P M T, most people are normal metabolizers, with only a small bar of poor metabolizers. N U D T fifteen comes later; its sub-Saharan African data are currently unknown.", "s": "pgxex:9", "r": null}, {"t": "Frequency together with the severity of the toxicity decides how much to worry about a variant, including whether to genotype everyone from a population. Azathioprine and six mercaptopurine are metabolized by T P M T. Only a few people may be T P M T poor metabolizers, but a poor metabolizer given the normal dose could die. She said, Those people need less than ten percent of the normal dose. So a rare phenotype can still justify genotyping, because the toxicity is severe.", "s": "pgxex:9", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l19-1": {"title": "When a metabolizing enzyme variant matters", "segs": [{"t": "Section eight. When a metabolizing enzyme variant matters. She said, Variations in our metabolizing enzymes are the biggest reason why we would see variation in drug response. A variant becomes clinically important in three cases. The enzyme is the major route that eliminates the drug or ends its action. The drug is a prodrug that needs activation. Or an altered pathway, or the parent drug itself, causes toxicity.", "s": "pgxex:12", "r": null}, {"t": "The direction of harm depends on whether the drug is already active or is a prodrug. A poor metabolizer cannot run the enzyme step. So an active drug is not removed and climbs toward toxicity, while a prodrug is never turned on and fails. An ultrarapid metabolizer runs the step too fast. So an active drug is gone before it works, and a prodrug is over activated, which can overdose the patient.", "s": "pgxex:14", "r": null}, {"t": "Clopidogrel shows that the busiest enzyme is not always the one that decides the outcome. It is a prodrug. About eighty five percent of a dose is inactivated by esterases, and only about fifteen percent is activated in two C Y P steps. Variants in C Y P two C nineteen most change how much active drug forms.", "s": "pgxex:15", "r": null}], "mins": 1}, "guide4-l19-2": {"title": "Butyrylcholinesterase and N acetyltransferase two", "segs": [{"t": "Section nine. The historical examples, butyrylcholinesterase and N acetyltransferase two. These were the first metabolizing enzymes in which polymorphisms were studied. Neither handles many drugs, but their genes were worked out early and a few drugs tie clearly to them. Each teaches one lesson. Butyrylcholinesterase shows how dangerous prolonged drug action can be. N acetyltransferase two shows that either direction of a phenotype can cause harm.", "s": "pgxex:13", "r": null}, {"t": "Butyrylcholinesterase hydrolyzes succinylcholine, a short acting muscle relaxant used in anesthesia. Succinylcholine paralyzes muscles, including the diaphragm. An extensive metabolizer clears it in about two minutes, so breathing returns quickly. A slow metabolizer keeps the drug for two to three hours, so the patient cannot breathe for that whole time unless the exposure is known. This phenotype is rare. She said, the frequency of being a slow metabolizer is only one in three thousand patients.", "s": "pgxex:18", "r": null}, {"t": "N acetyltransferase two is a phase two enzyme. It acetylates isoniazid, a tuberculosis drug, and it also handles procainamide. Patients are slow acetylators or fast acetylators, and both carry a risk. Slow acetylators keep isoniazid around and get peripheral nerve damage. On procainamide, slow acetylators can get drug induced lupus, because the drug is not acetylated fast enough. Fast acetylators make more of the isoniazid metabolites that cause hepatotoxicity. So you cannot assume only one phenotype is the dangerous one.", "s": "pgxex:19", "r": null}, {"t": "The phenotypes differ by population. About thirty to forty five percent of Caucasians are fast acetylators. About ten percent of Japanese people are slow. About eighty percent of the Middle Eastern group are slow, so that is the group to watch for slow acetylator problems.", "s": "pgxex:19", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l19-3": {"title": "C Y P two D six and codeine", "segs": [{"t": "Section ten. C Y P two D six, debrisoquine, and codeine. C Y P two D six was the first P four fifty enzyme studied in depth, and it is the best characterized. She said, It metabolizes about fifteen to twenty five percent of the medications on the market, so that's a lot of work that it does.", "s": "pgxex:20", "r": null}, {"t": "Debrisoquine is cleared almost entirely by C Y P two D six. So when people are grouped by the ratio of parent drug to metabolite, the trait is monogenic, meaning one gene drives it. The data separate into distinct peaks for ultrarapid, extensive, and poor metabolizers. A smooth bell curve would mean the opposite, that many genes blur the data together.", "s": "pgxex:21", "r": null}, {"t": "Each phenotype has a genotype behind it. Extra copies of the whole gene, written star two times N, make an ultrarapid metabolizer with very fast clearance or activation. Two wild type copies make an extensive, or normal, metabolizer, which is the common middle of the distribution. Star five over star five is a whole gene deletion, not an ultrarapid allele, so no enzyme is expressed and the person is a poor metabolizer.", "s": "pgxex:20", "r": null}, {"t": "More than one hundred alleles exist, but about nine account for over ninety five percent of phenotypes. They fall into three groups. Star one and star two are fully functional. Star three, four, five and six are nonfunctional. Star ten, seventeen and forty one have reduced function.", "s": "pgxex:22", "r": null}, {"t": "For a drug that C Y P two D six clears, a poor metabolizer accumulates it and has adverse effects at standard doses. For a prodrug it activates, a poor metabolizer makes too little active drug, and the therapy fails. Codeine is that kind of prodrug. She said, Codeine is metabolized by two D six to morphine. An ultrarapid metabolizer quickly turns a normal dose into a large amount of morphine, which can suppress breathing. Children died after codeine for cough, so codeine cough syrup is no longer given to kids. A super dose would only fit a drug that C Y P two D six clears, never codeine.", "s": null, "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l19-4": {"title": "C Y P two C nine and warfarin", "segs": [{"t": "Section eleven. C Y P two C nine, warfarin, and the narrow therapeutic index drugs. This enzyme matters because it metabolizes several drugs where a small change in clearance changes the outcome. She said, it does metabolism of a lot of our very narrow therapeutic index drugs, so things like warfarin that we've already talked to some about, and then phenytoin and glipizide. Tolbutamide is on the same list.", "s": "pgxex:23", "r": null}, {"t": "The two common variants both reduce function, and star three is the more severe loss. In her numbers, C Y P two C nine star two keeps about twenty percent of wild type activity, and star three keeps about five percent. The Katzung chapter states it differently, as a thirty to forty percent and an eighty to ninety percent reduction in S warfarin metabolism.", "s": "pgxex:23", "r": "katzung-5"}, {"t": "Warfarin is cleared by C Y P two C nine, so a low activity variant clears it slowly and the drug builds up. The starting dose must be lowered, not raised, to keep this anticoagulant out of the bleeding range.", "s": null, "r": null}, {"t": "Two separate studies point the same way. One measured clearance and found a gene dose effect. People with two copies of star three had about a ninety percent reduction in S warfarin clearance compared with people with two copies of star one. The other looked at who needed the lowest starting dose. The usual start is about five milligrams per day, and the lowest dose patients need about one point five milligrams per day. She said, at least eighty percent of the people in that dosing category have these low activity variants of two C nine. The dosing data strengthened the clearance data.", "s": "pgxex:24", "r": null}, {"t": "The variants are not equally common. About thirty percent of Caucasians carry a low activity variant, about one to three percent of African Americans, and about zero percent of East Asians in the population studied. C Y P two C nine is also inhibited by many drugs. An inhibitor lowers its activity, and a low activity variant lowers it further. The two add up rather than cancel, so a substrate can reach concentrations that are too high.", "s": "pgxex:23", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l19-5": {"title": "C Y P two C nineteen, clopidogrel, and proton pump inhibitors", "segs": [{"t": "Section twelve. C Y P two C nineteen, the star seventeen gain of function, clopidogrel, and proton pump inhibitors. This enzyme drives two opposite clinical stories. The point is to reason from variant, to enzyme function, to drug handling, to patient outcome. Its substrates are slightly acidic drugs, such as proton pump inhibitors, fluoxetine, sertraline, and diazepam. There are over thirty alleles, but four drive most phenotypes, which run from poor and intermediate to extensive and ultrarapid.", "s": "pgxex:25", "r": null}, {"t": "Star two and star three carry a premature stop codon, so no active enzyme is made. Star seventeen is the first gain of function variant in this lecture. She said, The reason for that is this snip is located in the upstream region of the gene where the machinery for expressing the gene sits down on the gene and starts its work. So more C Y P two C nineteen is made and total activity rises. Star seventeen does not change the enzyme's shape, and it is not extra gene copies.", "s": "pgxex:25", "r": null}, {"t": "Clopidogrel is a prodrug that C Y P two C nineteen helps activate, so both directions are dangerous. A star two or star three carrier makes too little active drug and keeps sticky platelets, which is therapeutic failure. A star seventeen carrier makes too much and is over anticoagulated. That is why she said, clopidogrel is a drug for which we should be genotyping people for their two C nineteen status.", "s": "pgxex:26", "r": null}, {"t": "With proton pump inhibitors, being a poor metabolizer is good. Low activity keeps drug levels higher, which raises gastric p H more and improves cure rates. This is safe because the therapeutic index of these drugs is very wide. She said, When you had the low activity version of the enzyme increasing drug blood concentrations, you had better cure rates. In the figure from Furuta and colleagues, two thousand four, which used lansoprazole, poor metabolizers had the highest area under the curve and the highest gastric p H, not the extensive metabolizers. The assigned reading, Furuta and colleagues in nineteen ninety eight, treated sixty two Japanese patients with omeprazole plus amoxicillin, and its authors state that plasma omeprazole levels were not measured.", "s": "pgxex:27", "r": "furuta-1998"}, {"t": "The same high levels are a problem with the other substrates. A poor metabolizer on a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor or on diazepam has more adverse drug responses, and more sedation with diazepam. So the poor metabolizer benefits only where the therapeutic index is wide, as with proton pump inhibitors.", "s": "pgxex:25", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l19-6": {"title": "D P D and five fluorouracil, U G T one A one and irinotecan", "segs": [{"t": "Section thirteen. Two oncology examples, D P D with five fluorouracil, and U G T one A one with irinotecan. Each adds its own twist. D P D is the main breakdown route that keeps five fluorouracil in check. U G T one A one is needed to clear a toxic active metabolite of a drug that is already active. In both cases, a low activity variant can cause severe bone marrow suppression.", "s": null, "r": null}, {"t": "D P D, dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase, is coded by the D P Y D gene. It handles the antimetabolites five fluorouracil, capecitabine, and tegafur. About eighty percent of a five fluorouracil dose is normally broken down by D P D to non toxic products. A low activity variant sends more drug down the route that makes the active cytotoxic metabolite. The worst result is myelosuppression that can be lethal. Mucositis, neurotoxicity, hand foot syndrome, and diarrhea can also occur. These low activity variants are not yet well characterized. She flagged this as a coming area.", "s": "pgxex:29", "r": null}, {"t": "Irinotecan is not a prodrug. It is already active, and it is further metabolized to an active metabolite, S N thirty eight. She said, what clears S N thirty eight from the body is U G T one A one. A carrier of U G T one A one star twenty eight has lower expression and less glucuronidation, so S N thirty eight builds up to toxic levels. Here the enzyme is needed to remove a toxic metabolite, not to make or inactivate the parent drug. The Katzung chapter differs and calls irinotecan a prodrug.", "s": "pgxex:30", "r": "katzung-5"}, {"t": "Compare U G T one A one star twenty eight with C Y P two C nineteen star seventeen. Both are promoter region variants. She said, this variant in the promoter region decreases the expression of this enzyme. Star seventeen does the opposite and increases expression. So a promoter variant can push expression either way, and each one's direction has to be known.", "s": "pgxex:30", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l19-7": {"title": "Thiopurine methyltransferase and the thiopurines", "segs": [{"t": "Section fourteen. Thiopurine methyltransferase, T P M T, and the thiopurines. There are two thiopurines, azathioprine and mercaptopurine. She said, Azathioprine, we use it as an immunosuppressant. It's a prodrug. The body converts azathioprine to six mercaptopurine. Mercaptopurine itself is given directly for cancer, because its kinetics suit that use. Low T P M T activity changes how much of either drug becomes active.", "s": "pgxex:31", "r": null}, {"t": "Six mercaptopurine has competing routes. Xanthine oxidase and T P M T turn it into inactive products. Another pathway turns it into the active six thioguanine nucleotides. These stop cancer cells from dividing, but they also suppress the bone marrow. So T P M T normally shunts part of the drug away from the active metabolite.", "s": "pgxex:32", "r": null}, {"t": "When T P M T activity is low, more drug goes down the pathway to the six thioguanine nucleotides. The extra active metabolite suppresses blood cells. Low T P M T does not make the drug fail. It makes toxicity rise. The metabolite that treats the cancer is the same one that kills blood cells, so a poor metabolizer on a standard dose risks severe immunosuppression and must be given much less drug.", "s": "pgxex:32", "r": null}, {"t": "Two S N Ps, single nucleotide polymorphisms, account for over ninety percent of the inactivating alleles. In Caucasians the common variant is T P M T star three A, made of two nonsynonymous S N Ps. In East Asians it is T P M T star three C, made of one S N P. Both are inactivating, not gain of function. The phenotypes are extensive, intermediate, and poor, and they are measured as enzyme activity in red blood cells.", "s": "pgxex:33", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l20-1": {"title": "T P M T and thiopurine dosing", "segs": [{"t": "Section fifteen. T P M T and genotype guided thiopurine dosing. T P M T, thiopurine methyltransferase, is the last metabolizing enzyme example, and here the dose is set by genotype. T P M T sends six mercaptopurine to an inactive product, six methylmercaptopurine. When T P M T activity is low, more of the dose becomes the active six thioguanine nucleotides, called six T G N. Six T G N stops dividing cells. Blood cells stop dividing too, so the white count drops, and a standard dose can cause fatal myelosuppression. So low T P M T does not make the drug fail. It means more active metabolite and more toxicity.", "s": "pgxex:32", "r": null}, {"t": "Hair loss and gastrointestinal distress happen for a related reason. Those cells are not cancerous, but they divide rapidly, and the drugs cannot tell them apart from cancer cells. The common variant alleles are star three A and star three C. Star three A, seen in Caucasians, is a haplotype of two non synonymous S N Ps. About five percent of people are heterozygous, and about one in three hundred are homozygous. Star three C, seen in East Asians, is one S N P in the coding region.", "s": "pgxex:33", "r": null}, {"t": "Before genotyping, the phenotype came from T P M T activity in red blood cells, and doses were adjusted from that. On the activity histogram, homozygous wild type people form the largest peak at the highest activity. Heterozygotes are a smaller intermediate group, and homozygous variant patients are a very small group with the lowest activity. So for T P M T, most people are wild type at the high activity end. She said, you can't assume that the wild type is always in the middle.", "s": "pgxex:34", "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "Wild type patients get the full mercaptopurine dose. Heterozygotes get about thirty five percent of the wild type dose. That is not half. She said, It's not half the dose, it's about thirty five percent is lower than half the dose. Homozygous deficient patients all need a reduction and tolerate less than ten percent of the wild type dose. The thiopurines were the first drugs for which the F D A held public hearings on putting pharmacogenetic information in the label. Genotyping first is now the standard of care, and she said you could be considered negligent if you did not know your patient's T P M T status.", "s": "pgxex:35", "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "The Cheok and Evans figure, from Nature Reviews Cancer in two thousand six, shows why the dose follows genotype. When everyone gets the same dose, variant variant patients have very high six T G N and the most toxicity. When doses are individualized, six T G N and toxicity are similar and low in all groups. Matching the dose to the genotype brings exposure back into one range.", "s": "pgxex:37", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l20-2": {"title": "P glycoprotein and drug transporters", "segs": [{"t": "Section sixteen. Drug transporters and P glycoprotein. Transporters are the second big area of pharmacogenomics, and P glycoprotein, or P G P, is the best characterized of them. It is an efflux transporter in the gut wall that pumps drugs out of the gut cells and back into the lumen. So when P G P works poorly, less drug is pumped back, and more of it is absorbed. That has the same effect as taking a P G P inhibitor. Lower P G P means more absorption, not less.", "s": "pgxex:40", "r": null}, {"t": "Transporters move drugs across the gut, excrete drugs into bile and urine, and carry drugs across the blood brain barrier. They are found in gut cells, called enterocytes, in liver cells, called hepatocytes, and in kidney proximal tubule cells. They sit on both the luminal side and the blood side, which is called basolateral. She said, Remember, B is for blood side. Basolateral is the blood side, not the gut side.", "s": "pgxex:41", "r": null}, {"t": "On the plasma and cellular concentration curves, the dashed line is the variant. With a low activity P G P, the drug stays in the gut cells, so the cellular concentration rises sharply, and more reaches the blood. She said, You will end up with higher bioavailability of drugs that are substrates for P G P. Increased intestinal P G P expression can limit absorption, while decreased expression may give supratherapeutic plasma concentrations.", "s": "pgxex:42", "r": null}, {"t": "P G P is coded by A B C B one, a gene also called M D R one, for multidrug resistance one. These are two names for one gene, and A B C B one is the newer name. She said, This transporter is not coded by two different genes. P G P affects the distribution of digoxin, cyclosporine, tacrolimus and the antiretroviral protease inhibitors. Digoxin is her main example because its therapeutic index is narrow. In a low activity P G P patient, a normal dose can push the heart rate far too low, so the dose would be adjusted. That is why you characterize transporters.", "s": "pgxex:45", "r": null}, {"t": "Three common S N Ps sit in exon twelve, C twelve thirty six T, exon twenty one, G twenty six seventy seven T, and exon twenty six, C thirty four thirty five T. Exons twenty one and twenty six are both linked to digoxin. Only exon twenty six is linked to protease inhibitor levels and clinical effects. In the initial studies, exon twenty one was not linked to the protease inhibitors. P G P is still hard to pin down. A variant's effect changes with the drug, different lab cell systems give different results, and the partner transporters on the blood side are not all characterized yet.", "s": "pgxex:46", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l20-3": {"title": "O A T P one B one and simvastatin", "segs": [{"t": "Section seventeen. O A T P one B one and simvastatin. O A T P one B one is the second transporter she covered, and a newer one than P glycoprotein. The gene is S L C O one B one, and the protein is O A T P one B one. It sits in the liver, on the hepatocyte sinusoidal membrane, not in the gut. It handles hepatic uptake of weakly acidic drugs and endogenous compounds, such as statins, methotrexate and bilirubin. At least forty non synonymous S N Ps are known.", "s": "pgxex:47", "r": null}, {"t": "Star five is one S N P, r s four one four nine zero five six, which is five twenty one T to C, valine one seventy four to alanine. It has reduced function, possibly from reduced expression, and it is rare, about one percent. Star fifteen and star seventeen are the common ones, five to fifteen percent in European and Asian populations. They are haplotypes that contain the star five S N P plus other S N Ps. She said, Remember, these two are haplotypes, which means they contain more than one snip. So any two of these alleles give two copies of the star five S N P, and count as homozygous.", "s": "pgxex:47", "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "O A T P one B one takes simvastatin into the liver. A reduced function transporter takes up less, so more simvastatin stays in the plasma. Patients homozygous for the star five S N P have a large increase in simvastatin plasma exposure, measured as area under the curve. The increase is two hundred twenty one percent, which she read aloud as two hundred twenty one times. Either way, the increase is large.", "s": "pgxex:47", "r": null}, {"t": "High statin levels cause muscle breakdown, and pain in the large muscles is the warning symptom. In rhabdomyolysis, the contents of the muscle cells enter the blood and damage the kidneys. That is why patients are now often genotyped for star five, fifteen and seventeen. For at least one nonfunctional allele, lower the simvastatin dose or give another statin. She said some other statins cause less rhabdomyolysis, so a different statin may be better than just a lower dose.", "s": "pgxex:47", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l20-4": {"title": "Drug target receptors", "segs": [{"t": "Section eighteen. Drug target receptors. Drug targets are the third area of pharmacogenomics. They are less well characterized than metabolizing enzymes, and she gave only what she considers good science. A target variant can change how well a drug works, as with the beta one receptor and metoprolol. Or a target variant can cause the disease itself, so the drug is chosen because it hits that variant, as with E G F R and gefitinib.", "s": null, "r": null}, {"t": "The beta one receptor gene is A D R B one. Its non synonymous S N Ps are serine forty nine to glycine, and arginine three eighty nine to glycine. The variant changes the receptor, so metoprolol may not bind as well. Patients homozygous for serine forty nine and arginine three eighty nine, the wild type, had the better response, not the variant carriers. So a patient with the variants may respond poorly, and genotype may guide the choice of starting drug.", "s": "pgxex:50", "r": null, "tb": 0}, {"t": "The beta two receptor gene is A D R B two, the target of beta two agonists such as albuterol. It has more than eleven S N Ps, and three are frequent, arginine sixteen to glycine, codon twenty seven, and the promoter. These are separate from the beta one S N Ps. Arginine sixteen to glycine is in beta two, not beta one.", "s": "pgxex:51", "r": null}, {"t": "At codon sixteen, A changes to G, so the codon A G A, for arginine, becomes G G A, for glycine. Arginine sixteen is called the wild type because it was identified first. The results for arginine sixteen to glycine are unclear. She said, This again, is not something that is likely to result in people being genotyped.", "s": "pgxex:52", "r": null}, {"t": "E G F R, also called H E R one or E R B B one, is the receptor for epidermal growth factor, which binds it and signals the cell to divide. In some non small cell lung cancers, a gain of function E G F R is overexpressed and drives division. Gefitinib binds the inside of the receptor and blocks the signal. Response was strongly tied to the mutation in two thousand four. Everyone has E G F R, but gefitinib works only when the tumor has the variant receptor, so it is used only then. She said, we're using this drug to assure efficacy, right? We're individualizing their care.", "s": "pgxex:53", "r": null}, {"t": "A very new area is the G proteins coupled to receptors. Variants, especially in the alpha subunit, are associated with the efficacy of some antidepressants. These variants sit in the G protein coupled to the receptor, not in the receptor itself. So a response that looks like it comes from the receptor may actually come from its G protein.", "s": "pgxex:54", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l20-5": {"title": "Drug target enzymes, ALOX5 and ACE", "segs": [{"t": "Section nineteen. Drug target enzymes, five lipoxygenase and A C E. Enzymes can be drug targets too, and both of her examples break the expected pattern. The A L O X five variant changes response to a drug without changing the disease. The A C E variant does not seem to change how well the drug works, but it probably changes the risk of adverse effects.", "s": "pgxex:55", "r": null}, {"t": "Five lipoxygenase, the A L O X five gene, has a role in asthma, and it is the target of five lipoxygenase inhibitors. The variant is a set of tandem repeats in the promoter. The five repeat allele is thought to increase expression. Only subjects with at least one five repeat allele responded to the inhibitor. The repeat alters the amount of the drug's target, so it changes how the inhibitor works. But it has no link to asthma severity. She said, We don't always need to have a disease being caused by that polymorphism to impact the treatment of it.", "s": "pgxex:55", "r": null}, {"t": "The A C E variant is not a single nucleotide polymorphism. It is an insertion or deletion, an InDel, in intron sixteen, and it is associated with plasma A C E levels. She said, The genetic variant for the A C E gene is not seeming to impact the efficacy of A C E inhibitors. This genetic variant is impacting the risk of side effects to the A C E inhibitors. So the variant is linked to adverse effects, not to efficacy.", "s": "pgxex:56", "r": null}, {"t": "Cough occurs in about ten percent of patients on A C E inhibitors. Angioedema occurs in only zero point one to zero point two percent. Because angioedema is so rare, a study would need a very large population to enroll enough cases to genotype, so the link is unproven. Until it is proven, genotyping cannot be used to keep at risk patients off the drug class. If it were proven, a patient could be genotyped for the A C E InDel, and a carrier's hypertension could be treated another way.", "s": "pgxex:56", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l20-6": {"title": "Warfarin, C Y P two C nine, V K O R C one and C Y P four F two", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty. Warfarin, the integrated example. Most drugs of genetic concern involve one gene at a time. Warfarin dosing depends on a metabolizing enzyme, C Y P two C nine, which is pharmacokinetics. It depends on its target, V K O R C one, which is pharmacodynamics. And it depends on C Y P four F two, which inactivates vitamin K. Body weight and gender also play a role in warfarin dosing.", "s": "pgxex:57", "r": null}, {"t": "Start with the mechanism. Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, so vitamin K epoxide is not reduced back to its active form. Gamma glutamyl carboxylase then cannot make active clotting factors. That is why vitamin K is the antidote in overdose.", "s": "pgxex:57", "r": null}, {"t": "Low activity in either gene lowers the dose. With a low activity C Y P two C nine, warfarin is cleared more slowly, so the same dose builds up higher. With a low activity V K O R C one, the target itself is less active, so less drug is needed to inhibit it. She said, If the target of the drug is not very active in one patient compared to another patient, you don't need so much of the drug in that patient that has the genetic variant.", "s": "pgxex:57", "r": null}, {"t": "When both occur together, she called it a double whammy. A patient wild type for both genes needs about five milligrams a day. C Y P two C nine star three star three with wild type V K O R C one needs less. Homozygous variant for both needs the lowest dose to prevent bleeding.", "s": "pgxex:57", "r": null}, {"t": "Warfarin has two enantiomers. S warfarin has the most activity and is cleared mainly by C Y P two C nine, which turns it into inactive seven hydroxy warfarin. R warfarin is weak and is cleared by multiple C Y Ps. Both act on V K O R C one. C Y P two C nine star two and star three do not abolish the enzyme. They reduce its activity, and star three is lowest. It is C Y P two C nineteen star two and star three that make no active enzyme.", "s": "pgxex:58", "r": null}, {"t": "C Y P four F two runs the other way. It metabolizes reduced, active vitamin K to an inactive form. When C Y P four F two activity is low, less active vitamin K is inactivated, so more active vitamin K is present. She said, Which means you've basically got more of the antidote for warfarin on board. So the patient needs more warfarin, not less. A more active C Y P four F two works the other way.", "s": "pgxex:58", "r": null}], "mins": 3}, "guide4-l20-7": {"title": "H L A genes and drug hypersensitivity", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty one. Immune system variants, the H L A genes and drug hypersensitivity. She called this area probably the hottest area of pharmacogenomics, and the newest, not the oldest. Many biologicals now on the market have immunologically based side effects, some of them autoimmune, and these are hard to predict. As more biologicals reach the market, these effects become more common, and more variants will be identified.", "s": "pgxex:59", "r": null}, {"t": "H L A molecules are receptors on antigen presenting cells. She said, They are the receptor that will present the antigen to your T cell. That starts an immune response. A variant may change the antigen binding site of the H L A molecule. The molecule may then recognize peptides that are not true antigens and start an immune response against them. So H L A variants change immune recognition. They are not metabolism or transport variants and do not change how the drug is handled. Polymorphisms in H L A B, H L A D Q and H L A D R have been associated with many drug induced hypersensitivity reactions.", "s": "pgxex:59", "r": null}, {"t": "Katzung Table five four pairs variants with drugs. The two she will spend time on are both H L A B. H L A B star fifty seven zero one goes with abacavir induced skin toxicity, which she said can be fatal. H L A B star fifteen zero two goes with carbamazepine induced skin toxicity, also potentially fatal. The table also lists H L A B star fifty eight zero one with allopurinol skin toxicity, and an H L A D R B one, D R B five and D Q B one haplotype with amoxicillin clavulanate liver injury. How common these variants are varies across populations and is not yet well defined.", "s": "pgxex:60", "r": null}], "mins": 2}, "guide4-l20-8": {"title": "The Furuta nineteen ninety eight paper", "segs": [{"t": "Section twenty two. The Furuta nineteen ninety eight discussion, C Y P two C nineteen, omeprazole, and H pylori cure. Furuta and colleagues compared H pylori cure rates by C Y P two C nineteen genotype. She used the paper to make students work from the paper's old allele labels to star nomenclature, and from genotype to enzyme activity. She said, You guys have to be able to drill down from your phenotype of your patient to the molecular structure of the D N A and know what is star two? What is star three? Why is that enzyme not active in this person?", "s": "pgxex:61", "r": null}, {"t": "The study enrolled sixty two Japanese patients, mostly middle aged men, with a gastric or duodenal ulcer and H pylori infection. They took omeprazole twenty milligrams for eight weeks for a gastric ulcer or six weeks for a duodenal ulcer, plus amoxicillin five hundred milligrams four times a day for two weeks. That is one P P I, a proton pump inhibitor, with one antibiotic. In the United States, H pylori is usually treated with triple therapy, a P P I with two antibiotics. The paper calls the enzyme S mephenytoin four prime hydroxylase, an older name for C Y P two C nineteen.", "s": null, "r": "furuta-1998"}, {"t": "In star nomenclature, wild type is star one, m one is star two, and m two is star three. Group one, star one star one, has normal activity and a cure rate of twenty eight point six percent. Group two carries one star one allele with star two or star three. It still makes some functional enzyme, and the cure rate was sixty percent. Group three carries two of the variant alleles, has no activity, and every patient was cured, one hundred percent. Star two and star three are S N Ps that create a premature stop codon, so the protein is too short to work. No patient was star three star three. Such a patient would simply belong in group three, so the gap only reflects who was recruited.", "s": null, "r": "furuta-1998", "tb": 0}, {"t": "The discussion section explains why poor metabolizers were cured more often. Omeprazole stays high, so stomach p H stays higher. Amoxicillin is unstable at low p H, so it is more stable at the higher p H. Omeprazole also helps get amoxicillin into the gastric juice, where H pylori is. She said group three had the highest A U C, but the paper did not measure plasma omeprazole levels. It cites relative A U Cs of one to three point seven to twenty from earlier work. This paper used omeprazole with amoxicillin. Lansoprazole was a different figure, from two thousand four.", "s": null, "r": "furuta-1998"}, {"t": "Can the result be applied in the United States? She said, Not on the face of it. Star two and star three are relatively frequent in Japanese people. The paper gives the poor metabolizer genotype as eighteen to twenty three percent in Japanese persons, versus three to five percent in white U S or European populations. With genotyping, the same response is expected and only the group sizes differ, so fewer antibiotics might be possible for the right genotypes.", "s": null, "r": "furuta-1998"}], "mins": 3}};
